insert into public.reading_passages (id, language, level, title, body, questions, sort_order, is_published)
values ('generated-reading-en-b1-b3ab1d0b309e', 'en', 'B1', 'Choices and Consequences: Understanding Conditionals', 'Life is full of choices, and every choice has a consequence. This idea is at the heart of conditional sentences, which we use to talk about what is true, what might happen, and what could be different. In English, there are three important types of conditionals: zero, first, and second conditional. Each one helps us express different relationships between conditions and results.

The zero conditional is used for facts and general truths. For example, “If you heat water to 100 degrees Celsius, it boils.” This sentence describes something that is always true. In this type of sentence, we use the present simple in both parts: “If” + present simple, present simple. This conditional is common in science and everyday routines. For instance, “If I drink coffee at night, I don’t sleep well.”

The first conditional talks about real and possible situations in the future. It is formed with “If” + present simple, and the result clause uses “will” + base verb. For example, “If it rains tomorrow, we will stay at home.” This structure is used when there is a real chance that the condition will happen. We often use it to make promises, warnings, or plans. For instance, “If you study hard, you will pass the exam.”

The second conditional talks about unreal or imaginary situations in the present or future. It is formed with “If” + past simple, and the result clause uses “would” + base verb. For example, “If I won the lottery, I would travel around the world.” This type of sentence is used for dreams, advice, and hypothetical situations. We also use it to give advice with the structure “If I were you, I would…” For example, “If I were you, I would take an umbrella.” Note that in the second conditional, we use “were” for all subjects in formal English, though many people say “was” in informal speech.

To help you remember the differences, think of it this way: zero conditional is for facts (always true), first conditional is for real possibilities (likely to happen), and second conditional is for unlikely or imaginary situations (not real now). Choosing the right conditional depends on how probable the situation is and how certain you are about the outcome.

Now, let’s see these conditionals in a real-life story. Imagine a Brazilian student named Lucas who wants to study in Canada. He knows that if he doesn’t improve his English, he won’t get a scholarship. So, he studies every day. If he practices speaking with native speakers, he becomes more confident. That is a zero conditional fact. He also plans: “If I save enough money, I will apply for a student visa.” That is a first conditional. But sometimes he dreams: “If I had more time, I would take extra classes.” That is a second conditional. Understanding conditionals helps Lucas make decisions and understand the world.

In conclusion, conditionals are powerful tools in English. They allow us to talk about facts, possibilities, and dreams. By mastering the zero, first, and second conditionals, you can express yourself more clearly and understand others better. Practice using them in your daily life, and soon they will become natural.', '[{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"O zero conditional é usado para fatos e verdades gerais, como ''If you heat water, it boils.''","options":["Facts and general truths","Imaginary situations","Future possibilities","Past events"],"prompt":"What is the zero conditional used for?"},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"O primeiro condicional usa ''if + presente simples'' e ''will + verbo'' para situações reais no futuro. A frase c é a única que segue essa estrutura.","options":["If I won the lottery, I would travel the world.","If you mix red and yellow, you get orange.","If it rains tomorrow, we will cancel the picnic.","If I were you, I would say sorry."],"prompt":"Which sentence is an example of the first conditional?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"O segundo condicional usa ''if + passado simples'' e ''would + verbo base'' para situações irreais ou imaginárias.","options":["If + present simple, present simple","If + past simple, would + base verb","If + present simple, will + base verb","If + past perfect, would have + past participle"],"prompt":"What structure does the second conditional use?"},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"Essa frase descreve uma verdade geral sobre o efeito do café, então é zero conditional (usando presente simples nas duas partes).","options":["First conditional","Second conditional","Zero conditional","It is not a conditional"],"prompt":"In the sentence ''If I drink coffee at night, I don''t sleep well,'' what type of conditional is it?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"A opção correta é ''If I had time, I would help you'' – segundo condicional para situação irreal no presente. As outras misturam tempos incorretamente.","options":["If I will have time, I will help you.","If I had time, I would help you.","If I have time, I would help you.","If I had time, I will help you."],"prompt":"Which sentence is correct?"},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"O segundo condicional expressa situações irreais ou imaginárias. Lucas não tem mais tempo agora, então é uma condição irreal.","options":["A real possibility in the future","A general truth","An unreal or imaginary situation","A past action"],"prompt":"What does Lucas''s sentence ''If I had more time, I would take extra classes'' express?"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
