insert into public.reading_passages (id, language, level, title, body, questions, sort_order, is_published)
values ('generated-reading-en-b1-19085f632d14', 'en', 'B1', 'Understanding Conditional Sentences in English', 'Conditional sentences are a fundamental part of English. They help us talk about possibilities, hypothetical situations, and their results. In this lesson, we will explore three important types: zero, first, and second conditionals.

First, the zero conditional is used for general truths or facts. It describes things that are always true if a certain condition is met. For example, "If you heat water to 100 degrees Celsius, it boils." In this structure, we use the present simple in both the ''if'' clause and the main clause. This conditional is common in scientific facts and habits.

Second, the first conditional is used for real and possible situations in the future. It talks about something that will likely happen if a condition is fulfilled. The structure is: if + present simple, will + base verb. For example, "If it rains tomorrow, we will stay at home." We can also use ''unless'' to mean ''if not'', as in "We will miss the train unless we hurry."

Third, the second conditional is used for unreal or imaginary situations in the present or future. It describes situations that are not true or are very unlikely. The structure is: if + past simple, would + base verb. For example, "If I won the lottery, I would travel the world." Here, we use ''were'' instead of ''was'' for all subjects, especially in formal English. For example, "If she were here, she would know what to do."

To help you understand better, let''s look at some examples. Zero conditional: "If you freeze water, it becomes ice." First conditional: "If you don''t study, you will fail the exam." Second conditional: "If I had more time, I would learn to play the guitar." These sentences show how conditionals express different levels of possibility.

Now, let''s practice with a short text. Imagine you are planning a trip to Brazil. You think about what you will do if you visit different places. If you go to Rio de Janeiro, you will see the Christ the Redeemer statue. If you have enough time, you will visit the Amazon rainforest. But if you didn''t have a lot of money, you would choose a cheaper destination. And if you were a nature lover, you would definitely go to the Pantanal. These decisions depend on your conditions.

Remember, conditionals are not difficult if you practice. Pay attention to the verb tenses and the meaning they convey. Try to use them in your daily conversations, and you will become more confident.

Now, answer the questions below to test your understanding.', '[{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"A zero conditional é usada para verdades gerais, e ambas as cláusulas usam o presente simples. ''If you heat ice, it melts'' expressa uma verdade geral.","options":["If I had more time, I would travel.","If you heat ice, it melts.","If she comes, we will go out.","If they studied, they would pass."],"prompt":"Which sentence is a zero conditional?"},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"Na first conditional, usamos if + presente simples + will + verbo. A opção ''If you study, you will pass'' segue essa estrutura corretamente.","options":["If you will study, you will pass.","If you studies, you will pass.","If you study, you will pass.","If you studied, you would pass."],"prompt":"Choose the correct first conditional sentence."},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"A second conditional expressa situações irreais ou improváveis no presente ou futuro, como em ''If I were rich, I would buy a car''.","options":["General truths","Real future possibilities","Unreal or unlikely present/future situations","Past events"],"prompt":"What does the second conditional express?"},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"A opção ''If I knew the answer, I would tell you'' usa passado simples (knew) e would, caracterizando a second conditional.","options":["If you mix red and yellow, you get orange.","If I see him, I''ll tell him.","If I knew the answer, I would tell you.","If it snows, we will stay home."],"prompt":"Which of the following is a second conditional sentence?"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"Na first conditional, usamos presente simples na cláusula if. Portanto, ''If she is free tomorrow, she will join us''.","options":["is","was","were","will be"],"prompt":"Complete: ''If she ____ free tomorrow, she will join us.''"},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"Na second conditional, usamos ''were'' para todas as pessoas, especialmente em contextos formais: ''If I were you, I would apologize''.","options":["am","was","were","be"],"prompt":"What is the correct form of the verb in the second conditional? ''If I ____ you, I would apologize.''"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
