insert into public.reading_passages (id, language, level, title, body, questions, sort_order, is_published)
values ('generated-reading-en-b1-7e4ab0bdcf65', 'en', 'B1', 'What Did She Say? An Introduction to Reported Speech', 'Have you ever told a friend what someone else said? For example, you might say, "Maria said she was tired," instead of repeating her exact words, "I am tired." This is called reported speech. It is a common way to share information in English. Reported speech helps you talk about conversations, news, and stories. In this lesson, you will learn the basics of how to change direct speech (exact words) into reported speech.

First, let''s look at an example from the news. Researchers once reported that children who are ambidextrous might face more challenges in school. They did not say, "Ambidextrous children have problems." Instead, they presented their findings using ''that''. You can say, "The researchers said that ambidextrous children were more likely to have problems." In reported speech, we often use ''that'' to link the reporting verb (said, told) with the reported information.

Usually, when you report what someone said, you adjust the tense of the verb. If the original statement is in the present simple, you often change it to the past simple. For example, if Ana says, "I love coffee," you can report: "Ana said she loved coffee." Notice that ''love'' becomes ''loved''. If the original is in the present continuous, like "I am studying," you change it to past continuous: "She said she was studying." This tense shift makes the reported speech sound more natural in English.

However, there are exceptions. If the reported information is still true, or if the reporting verb is in the present, you may not need to change the tense. For example, if John says, "The sun rises in the east," you can say, "John said that the sun rises in the east," because it''s a general truth. Also, if the reporting verb is in the present (e.g., ''She says''), the tense does not change: "She says she is hungry."

Another important change is in pronouns and time expressions. For example, if you report, "I will call you tomorrow," you might say, "He said he would call me the next day." ''Will'' changes to ''would'', ''you'' changes to ''me'', and ''tomorrow'' changes to ''the next day''. These changes help the listener understand the time and people correctly in the new context.

Let''s practice with a short story. Imagine that a friend tells you, "I am going to move to a new apartment." Later, you tell another friend: "My friend said she was going to move to a new apartment." This is reported speech. You are not using her exact words, but you are reporting the meaning. Reported speech is used in many situations, like telling news, sharing rumors, or retelling what a teacher said.

Now, let''s review the key points. When we use reported speech, we often change the tense of the verb, especially when the reporting verb is in the past. We also change pronouns and time expressions. But remember, if the fact is still true, we can keep the original tense. Also, we often use ''that'' after the reporting verb. Reported speech is a useful skill for English learners because it helps you communicate what others have said in a clear way.

Finally, practice is important. Listen to English conversations, read news articles, and try to report what people say. With time, you will become more comfortable using reported speech. Remember, it''s not just about repeating words; it''s about conveying meaning accurately. Next time you hear a good story, try telling it to a friend using reported speech. You''ll see how useful it is!', '[{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Reported speech é usado para relatar o que alguém disse, mas não necessariamente com as palavras exatas. Você transmite o significado.","options":["To repeat exact words without changes","To share what someone said in your own words","To create new conversations","To translate languages"],"prompt":"What is reported speech used for?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Quando o verbo de reportagem está no passado, geralmente mudamos o presente ''am'' para o passado ''was''.","options":["He said he is tired.","He said he was tired.","He said I was tired.","He said he tired."],"prompt":"If someone says ''I am tired,'' which reported speech is correct?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Se a informação ainda é verdade (por exemplo, um fato científico), podemos manter o tempo original.","options":["Because it is easier to say","Because the information is still true","Because the reporting verb is in the past","Because we don''t know the exact words"],"prompt":"Why do we sometimes NOT change the tense in reported speech?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"O pronome ''you'' pode mudar para ''me'', ''him'', etc., dependendo de quem está falando.","options":["It always stays ''you''","It changes to ''me'' or ''him'' depending on the context","It becomes ''they''","It is removed"],"prompt":"What happens to the pronoun ''you'' in reported speech?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Em reported speech, ''will'' vira ''would'' e ''tomorrow'' pode virar ''the next day''.","options":["He said he will call me tomorrow.","He said he would call me the next day.","He said he would call you tomorrow.","He said I will call you tomorrow."],"prompt":"Which sentence correctly reports ''I will call you tomorrow''?"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"No discurso relatado, o presente ''are'' muda para o passado ''were''. A frase original provavelmente usava o presente.","options":["Ambidextrous children are more likely to have school problems.","Ambidextrous children were more likely to have school problems.","Ambidextrous children have school problems.","Ambidextrous children had school problems."],"prompt":"The researchers said that ambidextrous children were more likely to have school problems. What was the original statement?"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
on conflict (id) do update set title=excluded.title, body=excluded.body, questions=excluded.questions, is_published=excluded.is_published, updated_at=now();

insert into public.reading_passages (id, language, level, title, body, questions, sort_order, is_published)
values ('generated-reading-en-b1-1adbe0112ee4', 'en', 'B1', 'The Rituals of Home', 'For many people, home is more than just a place to live. It is a sanctuary where we can relax, recharge, and be ourselves. But what makes a house a home? The answer may lie in the little things we do every day, often without thinking. Consider the simple act of making coffee in the morning. Some people enjoy grinding the beans, while others prefer the convenience of a pod. Both approaches are valid, but they reflect different priorities: the ritual versus the speed. When we choose to spend time on a ritual, we are often seeking a sense of control and mindfulness in our busy lives. On the other hand, choosing convenience might indicate that we value efficiency and have many demands on our time.

Similarly, the way we organize our living spaces can reveal a lot about our personalities. Some of us like to keep things tidy and minimal, while others find comfort in having our favorite books and photos around. There is no right or wrong, but understanding our own preferences can help us create an environment that truly feels like home. For example, a person who prefers a minimalist space might enjoy the process of decluttering, whereas someone who surrounds themselves with memories might find joy in displaying personal items. These choices not only reflect who we are but also influence how we feel in our homes.

Another aspect is the meals we prepare. Cooking a meal from scratch can be a rewarding experience, but it requires planning and effort. Ordering takeout is easy, but it might not give us the same satisfaction. The choice often depends on our mood and schedule. For instance, on a busy weeknight, we might prefer to order pizza rather than spending an hour in the kitchen. However, on a lazy Sunday, we might enjoy the process of baking bread. The key is to listen to ourselves and what we need at the moment. By doing so, we can make decisions that truly serve us.

Finally, consider the decision to invite friends over. Hosting a dinner party involves cleaning, cooking, and anticipating our guests'' needs. It can be stressful, but many people still choose to do it because the joy of sharing a meal with loved ones outweighs the effort. In contrast, meeting at a restaurant is simpler, but it may not offer the same intimacy. When we host, we create a space for connection and warmth that is hard to replicate elsewhere. This choice, too, reflects our desire for closeness.

Ultimately, our choices about home and hospitality are a reflection of our values. By being aware of these choices, we can make our homes more comfortable and our lives more meaningful. Whether we prefer the ritual of making coffee or the convenience of a pod, the way we arrange our rooms, or the meals we share, each decision shapes our sense of home. So, next time you find yourself in your daily routine, take a moment to consider what those little actions say about you and what you truly value.', '[{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"O texto sugere que a escolha entre ritual e conveniência reflete prioridades diferentes, como controle e atenção plena versus eficiência. Portanto, reflete nossas prioridades e valores.","options":["Our taste in coffee","Our time management skills","Our priorities and values","Our level of energy"],"prompt":"According to the text, what does the act of making coffee in the morning reflect?"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"O texto afirma que a forma como organizamos nossos espaços pode revelar muito sobre nossas personalidades, como preferir ordem ou manter objetos queridos por perto.","options":["It reveals our personality","It is a waste of time","It should be done by professionals","It is a difficult task"],"prompt":"What does the text say about organizing our living spaces?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"O texto menciona que em uma noite de semana corrida, podemos preferir pedir pizza a passar uma hora na cozinha, indicando que a escolha é para economizar tempo.","options":["They enjoy cooking","They want to save time","They have a date","They dislike restaurants"],"prompt":"According to the text, why might someone choose to order takeout on a busy weeknight?"},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"O texto afirma que muitas pessoas ainda recebem amigos porque a alegria de compartilhar uma refeição com entes queridos supera o esforço, ou seja, o prazer da companhia.","options":["To show off their cooking","To impress their friends","To enjoy sharing a meal with loved ones","To avoid going to restaurants"],"prompt":"What is the main reason some people still host dinner parties despite the stress?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"O texto conclui que nossas escolhas sobre lar e hospitalidade são um reflexo de nossos valores, destacando a importância da autoconsciência.","options":["They are often random","They are a reflection of our values","They are influenced by our friends","They are based on cost"],"prompt":"What does the text suggest about our choices regarding home and hospitality?"},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"O texto discute vários aspectos do dia a dia, como fazer café, organizar espaços, cozinhar e receber amigos, para mostrar como essas pequenas escolhas refletem nossos valores e criam um lar.","options":["How to make coffee properly","The importance of a clean house","The little decisions that make a house a home","The cost of hosting dinner parties"],"prompt":"What is the main idea of the text?"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
