insert into public.reading_passages (id, language, level, title, body, questions, sort_order, is_published)
values ('generated-reading-en-b1-cd05a2c87aba', 'en', 'B1', 'Choices and Consequences: Understanding Conditionals', 'Imagine you are planning a trip to a new city. You have two options: staying in a hotel downtown or renting an apartment near the airport. The decision you make will affect your experience. If you stay downtown, you will be close to museums and restaurants. But if you rent near the airport, you will save money on transportation. These are examples of real situations and their likely results. In English, we use the first conditional to talk about possible future events and their consequences. For instance, "If you book early, you will get a discount." This structure helps us plan and make decisions.

Now, think about your daily routine. What happens when you feel tired? If you don''t sleep well, you feel sleepy the next day. This is a general truth, something that always happens. For these situations, we use the zero conditional: "If you heat ice, it melts." It describes facts and habits. In Brazil, if it rains a lot, streets in some cities flood, so people stay home. This is a general pattern.

But what about imaginary situations? Suppose you had a million dollars. What would you do? You might travel the world or buy a house. These are unreal or unlikely situations, and we use the second conditional: "If I had a million dollars, I would travel the world." This structure allows us to dream and consider alternatives. In Brazil, if I won the lottery, I would invest in education.

Let''s look at a real news story: a judge delayed a decision about a school exit exam. The students who didn''t pass the exam would not graduate. The judge said he would decide later. This shows a real situation with possible outcomes. We can use the first conditional to talk about it: "If the judge allows the exam, students will graduate." Or we can imagine an alternative: "If the judge had decided earlier, students would have known sooner." The second conditional can express these unreal ideas.

Another story: a state legalized homebrewing. This means people can make their own beer if they follow certain rules. We use the first conditional for rules: "If you produce less than 100 gallons, you don''t need a license." But in the past, it was illegal. If someone brewed beer before the law, they would have been breaking the law. That''s a second conditional about a hypothetical past.

Finally, consider research on ambidextrous children. They are more likely to have problems at school. If a child is ambidextrous, they may struggle with language. This is a general truth, so we use the zero conditional: "If a child is ambidextrous, they have more difficulties." But we can also imagine: "If I were ambidextrous, I would practice writing with both hands." This is second conditional.

In summary, conditionals help us express real possibilities, general truths, and imaginary situations. By using them, you can make your English more precise and interesting. Now, let''s test your understanding with some questions.', '[{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"O zero conditional é usado para verdades gerais e fatos, como ''If you heat ice, it melts''.","options":["First conditional","Second conditional","Zero conditional","Third conditional"],"prompt":"Which conditional is used to talk about general truths or facts?"},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"O first conditional é usado para eventos futuros possíveis, como ''If it rains, we will stay home''.","options":["Impossible situations","General truths","Possible future events","Past regrets"],"prompt":"What does the first conditional express?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"O second conditional usa ''if + past simple'' e ''would + base form'', como ''If I had time, I would call you''.","options":["If I have time, I will call you.","If I had time, I would call you.","If I have time, I would call you.","If I had time, I will call you."],"prompt":"Choose the correct sentence using the second conditional."},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"O zero conditional usa ''if + present simple'' e ''present simple'', como ''If you study, you pass''.","options":["If you will study, you will pass.","If you study, you pass.","If you studied, you would pass.","If you had studied, you would have passed."],"prompt":"Which sentence is an example of the zero conditional?"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"No first conditional, usamos ''if + present simple'', então ''removes'' é correto.","options":["removes","removed","would remove","will remove"],"prompt":"Complete the sentence: ''If the judge ________ the exam requirement, students will graduate.''"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"O second conditional expressa situações imaginárias: ''If I won the lottery, I would travel around the world''.","options":["If I win the lottery, I will travel around the world.","If I won the lottery, I would travel around the world.","If I had won the lottery, I would have traveled around the world.","If I would win the lottery, I would travel around the world."],"prompt":"What is the correct second conditional sentence for this idea: ''win the lottery, travel around the world''?"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
