insert into public.reading_passages (id, language, level, title, body, questions, sort_order, is_published)
values ('generated-reading-en-b1-cda69197ef7b', 'en', 'B1', 'What Did She Say? An Introduction to Reported Speech', 'Ana and João were chatting in the living room. “I love this song!” Ana said. “Did you hear what she said?” João asked. “She said she loved that song,” his mother replied from the kitchen. This simple exchange is an example of reported speech, a way to tell someone what another person said without using their exact words. It’s very common in everyday conversations, especially when you’re telling a story or sharing news.

When we use reported speech, we often change the tense of the verb. For example, if someone says, “I am tired,” you might report: “She said she was tired.” The present tense ‘am’ becomes past ‘was’. This can be tricky, but with practice, it becomes natural. Another important change is with pronouns. If Ana says, “I love this song,” and you’re telling João, you might say, “Ana said she loved that song.” The pronoun ‘I’ changes to ‘she’, and ‘this’ often becomes ‘that’.

Let’s look at some more examples. Imagine your friend says, “I will call you later.” You can report this as, “He said he would call me later.” Notice how ‘will’ changes to ‘would’. Similarly, if someone says, “I have finished my homework,” you might report, “She said she had finished her homework.” The present perfect ‘have finished’ becomes past perfect ‘had finished’.

Reported speech is used in many situations. You might use it to share a message, to gossip, or to talk about what someone said in a meeting. For instance, at work, your boss might say, “We need to finish the project by Friday.” You could tell a colleague, “The boss said we needed to finish the project by Friday.” This way, you’re not quoting exactly, but you’re conveying the same idea.

One common mistake is forgetting to change the tense or pronoun. For example, saying, “She said she is tired” instead of “She said she was tired.” Also, remember that you don’t need to report every detail. You can summarize. For example, if someone said, “I went to the store, bought some milk, and then came home,” you could say, “She said she went to the store and came home.”

In Brazilian Portuguese, we also have reported speech, but the rules are slightly different. So, pay attention to the differences. Practice by listening to how native speakers report speech in movies, songs, and conversations. The more you practice, the more natural it becomes. Now, let’s test your understanding with a few questions.', '[{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Discurso reportado é uma forma de contar o que alguém disse sem usar as palavras exatas, geralmente mudando os tempos verbais e pronomes.","options":["A way to repeat someone''s exact words.","A way to tell someone what another person said without using exact words.","A type of speech that is always formal.","A speech that is only used in writing."],"prompt":"What is reported speech?"},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"No discurso reportado, o tempo verbal geralmente volta um passo no passado, como de presente para passado simples.","options":["It stays the same.","It changes to future tense.","It often changes to a past tense.","It changes to present tense."],"prompt":"In reported speech, what often happens to the verb tense?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"A forma correta muda ''am'' para ''was'' porque estamos reportando no passado.","options":["She said she is tired.","She said she was tired.","She said I am tired.","She said she has been tired."],"prompt":"Choose the correct reported speech for: ''I am tired.''"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"Muitas vezes, pronomes de primeira pessoa (I, my) mudam para terceira pessoa (he, she) no discurso reportado.","options":["First person pronouns change to third person.","Second person pronouns always remain the same.","Third person pronouns change to first person.","No pronoun changes are needed."],"prompt":"Which pronoun change is common in reported speech?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"No discurso reportado, ''will'' geralmente muda para ''would''.","options":["He said he will call me later.","He said he would call me later.","He said he calls me later.","He said he called me later."],"prompt":"If your friend said, ''I will call you later,'' how would you report it?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Um erro comum é esquecer de mudar o pronome, por exemplo, manter ''I'' em vez de mudar para ''he'' ou ''she''.","options":["Changing the tense too much.","Not changing the pronoun.","Changing the word order.","Adding extra information."],"prompt":"What is a common mistake in reported speech?"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
