insert into public.reading_passages (id, language, level, title, body, questions, sort_order, is_published)
values ('generated-reading-en-b1-34cdb50ba88d', 'en', 'B1', 'Daily Decisions: To Do or Doing?', 'Every day, we face decisions. Some are small, like choosing what to eat for lunch. Others are bigger, like deciding whether to continue studying or to start working. The way we talk about these choices often depends on using verbs in special ways. In English, we can use the base form of a verb with ''to'' (like ''to study'') or the ''-ing'' form (like ''studying''). This can be confusing, but it''s important to get it right.

Consider the verb ''remember''. When we say ''I remember to lock the door'', we mean we don''t forget to do it. But when we say ''I remember locking the door'', we are talking about a memory of doing it. The meaning changes completely! This is a common challenge for learners. Another verb with a similar change is ''stop''. ''I stopped to smoke'' means I paused an activity in order to smoke. But ''I stopped smoking'' means I quit the habit. So, the choice of form changes the message.

Sometimes, the meaning stays the same. For example, ''I like to swim'' and ''I like swimming'' both express enjoyment. However, with verbs like ''love'', ''hate'', and ''prefer'', the meaning is similar, but the -ing form is more common in British English, while American English often uses both. There are also verbs that are followed only by the -ing form, like ''enjoy'', ''mind'', and ''suggest''. We say ''I enjoy playing football'', not ''I enjoy to play''. On the other hand, some verbs are followed only by the infinitive, like ''want'', ''decide'', and ''hope''. We say ''I want to go'', not ''I want going''.

Understanding these patterns helps you express yourself clearly. In Brazil, where you might use ''lembrar de'' or ''parar de'', the idea is similar but not identical. For instance, ''lembrar de fazer'' is like ''remember to do'', while ''lembrar de ter feito'' is closer to ''remember doing''. Pay attention to the context to choose the right form.

Let''s practice with a story. Ana is a student from São Paulo. She wants to improve her English. She decided to study abroad next year. She enjoys learning new languages, but she hates taking tests. Last week, she stopped to think about her options. She considered studying in Canada or Australia. Finally, she chose to apply to a university in Toronto. She hopes to get a scholarship. She also plans to work part-time. She knows she needs to practice speaking every day. She doesn''t mind making mistakes because she believes in learning from them.

Now, think about your own decisions. Do you like to cook or do you prefer eating out? Do you remember turning off the lights when you leave? These small choices matter. By mastering verb patterns, you can share your experiences accurately. So, keep practicing. Remember to review the rules and try to use them in your daily conversations. You might find it challenging at first, but with time, it will become natural.', '[{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"''Remember to do'' significa lembrar de fazer algo, ou seja, não esquecer. A opção correta é ''I don''t forget to lock the door''.","options":["I have a memory of locking the door.","I forget to lock the door.","I don''t forget to lock the door.","I am locking the door now."],"prompt":"What does ''I remember to lock the door'' mean?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"''Stop doing'' (parar de fazer) indica que a pessoa abandonou o hábito. ''Stop to do'' (parar para fazer) indica uma pausa para fazer algo.","options":["I stopped to smoke.","I stopped smoking.","I stopped and smoked.","I stopped for a smoke."],"prompt":"Which sentence means the person quit smoking?"},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"O verbo ''enjoy'' é sempre seguido de -ing: ''enjoy doing''. ''Want'', ''decide'' e ''hope'' são seguidos de infinitivo.","options":["want","decide","enjoy","hope"],"prompt":"Which verb is followed only by the -ing form?"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"Com ''like'', as duas formas são usadas com o mesmo sentido. A diferença é sutil e não afeta o significado.","options":["There is no difference.","''Like to swim'' is for habits, ''like swimming'' is for general likes.","''Like swimming'' is more common in American English.","The meaning is completely different."],"prompt":"What is the difference between ''like to swim'' and ''like swimming''?"},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"''Decide'' é seguido de infinitivo: ''decide to do''. As outras opções estão incorretas: ''want'' + infinitivo, ''enjoy'' + -ing, ''suggest'' + -ing.","options":["I want going to the beach.","I enjoy to play soccer.","I decided to study hard.","She suggested to go out."],"prompt":"Which sentence is correct?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"''Stop to think'' indica que ela parou com o propósito de pensar. A história diz: ''she stopped to think about her options''.","options":["Because she wanted to stop studying.","Because she wanted to think about her options.","Because she was tired of thinking.","Because she decided to go to Canada."],"prompt":"In the story, why did Ana stop to think?"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
