insert into public.reading_passages (id, language, level, title, body, questions, sort_order, is_published)
values ('generated-reading-en-b1-2fa5902d1ddd', 'en', 'B1', 'Everyday Phrasal Verbs in Action', 'Phrasal verbs are combinations of a verb and a preposition or adverb that together have a special meaning. They are very common in English, especially in spoken language. Learning them can help you sound more natural and understand everyday conversations better. In this article, we will look at six common phrasal verbs: ''come across'', ''get over'', ''look forward to'', ''put off'', ''turn off'', and ''work out''. We will see how they are used in real-life situations.

First, consider the phrasal verb ''come across''. It means to find something or meet someone by chance. For example, you might come across an old photo while cleaning your room. Or you might come across a great article on the internet. This phrasal verb is very useful when you want to talk about unexpected discoveries.

Next, ''get over'' means to recover from something, like an illness or a difficult experience. After you have the flu, you need time to get over it. If you have a fight with a friend, you might need a few days to get over the bad feelings. Getting over challenges is part of life, and this phrasal verb helps you talk about that process.

Now, let''s talk about ''look forward to''. This means to be excited about something that is going to happen in the future. You might look forward to the weekend, or to a vacation, or to seeing a friend you haven''t seen in a long time. It is always followed by a noun or an -ing form. For example, ''I look forward to hearing from you'' is a common way to end a letter or email.

Another useful phrasal verb is ''put off''. It means to delay something or to make someone not want to do something. If you put off a task, you postpone it to a later time. For example, ''I keep putting off cleaning the garage.'' It can also mean to discourage someone. For instance, ''The bad weather put me off from going outside.'' This phrasal verb is handy when talking about procrastination.

Then, there''s ''turn off''. It can mean to switch off a device, like a light or a TV. But it also means to make someone lose interest or feel bored. For example, ''The long lecture turned me off the subject.'' This double meaning makes it interesting to learn.

Finally, ''work out'' has multiple meanings. It can mean to exercise, like going to the gym to work out. It can also mean to solve a problem or to find a solution. For instance, ''We need to work out a plan.'' Or it can mean to end well, as in ''Things worked out in the end.'' This phrasal verb is very versatile.

Understanding phrasal verbs like these will help you in many situations. Practice using them in your daily life. Try to make sentences with each one. The more you use them, the more natural they will become. Remember, making mistakes is a part of learning, so don''t be afraid to experiment. With time and practice, you''ll be using phrasal verbs like a native speaker.', '[{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"''Come across'' significa encontrar algo ou alguém por acaso, como no texto: ''you might come across an old photo while cleaning your room''.","options":["To find something by chance","To run very fast","To arrive at a place","To cross a street"],"prompt":"What does ''come across'' mean in the text?"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"O texto diz: ''After you have the flu, you need time to get over it.'' Portanto, ''get over'' é usado para se recuperar de uma doença.","options":["Get over it","Put it off","Come across it","Work it out"],"prompt":"If you are sick, what do you need to do?"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"''Look forward to'' significa estar animado com algo que vai acontecer no futuro, como no exemplo: ''I look forward to hearing from you''.","options":["To be excited about something in the future","To look for something","To wait for someone","To see something in front of you"],"prompt":"What does ''look forward to'' mean?"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"''Put off'' significa adiar ou procrastinar, como no exemplo: ''I keep putting off cleaning the garage.''","options":["putting it off","turning it off","getting it over","working it out"],"prompt":"If you delay doing your homework, you are..."},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"O texto diz: ''It can mean to switch off a device, like a light or a TV.'' Portanto, ''turn off'' é desligar.","options":["To switch it on","To switch it off","To break it","To lose it"],"prompt":"What does ''turn off'' mean when talking about a device?"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"O texto diz: ''It can mean to exercise, like going to the gym to work out.'' Portanto, ''work out'' pode significar malhar.","options":["work out","put off","come across","get over"],"prompt":"Which phrasal verb can mean ''to exercise''?"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
on conflict (id) do update set title=excluded.title, body=excluded.body, questions=excluded.questions, is_published=excluded.is_published, updated_at=now();

insert into public.reading_passages (id, language, level, title, body, questions, sort_order, is_published)
values ('generated-reading-en-b1-7c1126743c39', 'en', 'B1', 'If You Study, You Will Succeed: Understanding Conditionals', 'Conditional sentences are everywhere in English. They help us talk about possibilities, facts, and imaginary situations. For Brazilian learners at B1 level, mastering the zero, first, and second conditionals is a big step. Let''s explore them with real-life examples.

First, the zero conditional is used for general truths or habits. If you heat water to 100 degrees Celsius, it boils. This structure uses ''if'' + present simple, and the result is also in present simple. We often use it for scientific facts or things that are always true. For example, if I drink coffee at night, I don''t sleep well. It''s about cause and effect.

Second, the first conditional talks about possible future events. If it rains tomorrow, we will cancel the picnic. Here, we use ''if'' + present simple, and ''will'' + base verb. This is for real possibilities. For instance, if you study hard, you will pass the exam. It''s a promise or a warning about something likely to happen.

Third, the second conditional is for unreal or imaginary situations. If I won the lottery, I would travel around the world. The structure is ''if'' + past simple, and ''would'' + base verb. This is for things that are unlikely or impossible now. For example, if I had more time, I would learn to play the guitar. But I don''t have time, so it''s imaginary.

Now, let''s see how these are used in daily life. Imagine a friend asks for advice. You might say, ''If you feel tired, go to bed early'' (zero conditional, good advice). Or, ''If you miss the bus, you will be late for school'' (first conditional, a future possibility). Or, ''If I were you, I would talk to the teacher'' (second conditional, giving advice about an unreal situation).

But be careful: common mistakes include mixing the tenses. For example, saying ''If I will have time, I call you'' is wrong. It should be ''If I have time, I will call you.'' Also, forgetting to use ''were'' instead of ''was'' in second conditional with ''I'', as in ''If I were you.'' Practice makes perfect!

Let''s put this into context. Maria is a student in São Paulo. She has a big test next week. She says, ''If I study every day, I will pass.'' (first conditional). Her mother says, ''If you study, you usually do well.'' (zero conditional). Maria thinks, ''If I had more time, I would study more, but I work too much.'' (second conditional). So, she makes a plan: ''If I wake up early, I will study for an hour before work.'' (first conditional).

To remember: zero for facts, first for real future, second for unreal. Now, answer the questions below to test your understanding.', '[{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"O zero conditional é usado para verdades gerais e hábitos, como fatos científicos ou ações rotineiras.","options":["Possible future events","General truths and habits","Imaginary situations","Past actions"],"prompt":"What is the zero conditional used for?"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"A primeira condicional usa ''if'' + presente simples e ''will'' para futuro possível. A opção A está correta.","options":["If it rains, we will stay home.","If I had money, I would buy a car.","If you heat ice, it melts.","If I were you, I would go."],"prompt":"Choose the correct first conditional sentence:"},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"A segunda condicional usa ''if'' + passado simples e ''would'' para situações irreais. ''If I were rich, I would travel'' é um exemplo.","options":["If you mix red and yellow, you get orange.","If she comes, we will start.","If I were rich, I would travel.","If he works hard, he succeeds."],"prompt":"Which sentence is an example of the second conditional?"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"Na primeira condicional, não usamos ''will'' na cláusula ''if''; usamos o presente simples. O correto é ''If I have time''.","options":["It should be ''If I have time''","It should be ''If I had time''","It should be ''If I would have time''","There is no mistake"],"prompt":"Identify the mistake in this sentence: ''If I will have time, I will call you.''"},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"No second conditional, usamos ''would'' + verbo base. Portanto, ''would get'' é a resposta certa.","options":["will get","gets","would get","would have got"],"prompt":"Complete: ''If she studied more, she ___ better grades.''"},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"Essa é uma verdade científica, portanto é um zero conditional, usado para fatos gerais.","options":["A possible future action","An imaginary situation","A general truth","A past event"],"prompt":"What does the sentence ''If you heat water to 100°C, it boils'' express?"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
on conflict (id) do update set title=excluded.title, body=excluded.body, questions=excluded.questions, is_published=excluded.is_published, updated_at=now();

insert into public.reading_passages (id, language, level, title, body, questions, sort_order, is_published)
values ('generated-reading-en-b1-1e85b101b90b', 'en', 'B1', 'Everyday Phrasal Verbs: Getting By in Daily Life', 'Phrasal verbs are everywhere in English. They are combinations of a verb and a particle (like ''up'', ''out'', or ''off'') that together have a special meaning. For example, ''pick up'' can mean to lift something, but it can also mean to collect someone. Learning phrasal verbs is important for understanding and being understood in everyday conversations.

Imagine you are at a friend''s house. You need to leave early. You might say, "I have to go now, I need to pick up my sister from school." Here, ''pick up'' means to go and get someone in a car. Another common phrasal verb is ''look after''. If you say, "Can you look after my cat while I''m away?" you are asking someone to take care of your cat.

When you are traveling, you often have to ''check in'' at the airport. This means to register your arrival and get your boarding pass. At a hotel, you also ''check in'' to get your room key. Later, you ''check out'' when you leave and pay the bill. On the phone, if you can''t hear clearly, you might say, "Could you speak up?" This means to speak louder. That''s another phrasal verb: ''speak up''.

In the office, you might ''put off'' a meeting, which means to delay it. Or you might ''put off'' doing a task because you don''t want to do it. You could also ''take up'' a new hobby, meaning to start it. For example, "I''ve taken up painting." When you finish a project, you ''wrap up'' the details, meaning to finish them.

At home, you often ''throw away'' garbage, which means to discard it. You might also ''try on'' clothes before buying them, to see if they fit. If you are looking for something, you ''look for'' it. And if you find it, you might ''pick it up'' from the floor.

Phrasal verbs can be confusing because their meanings are not always obvious from the words. But with practice, they become natural. Start by learning the most common ones and use them in your daily conversations. Soon you''ll find you can ''get by'' (manage) in English more easily.

Remember, phrasal verbs are often used in informal settings, but they are also common in business and academic English. So, don''t be shy to use them. Just pay attention to the context and the particle. In time, you''ll ''pick up'' new phrasal verbs without even thinking!

Now, let''s test what you''ve learned with a few questions.', '[{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"No contexto da frase, ''pick up'' significa ir buscar alguém, ou seja, coletar a pessoa em um lugar. As outras opções são significados possíveis de ''pick up'', mas não se aplicam aqui.","options":["To lift something heavy","To go and collect someone","To learn something quickly","To answer the phone"],"prompt":"What does ''pick up'' mean in the sentence: \"I need to pick up my sister from school.\"?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"''Look after'' significa cuidar de algo ou alguém. A opção correta é ''You take care of it'', que é a definição de cuidar.","options":["You search for it.","You take care of it.","You watch it play.","You feed it twice a day."],"prompt":"If you ''look after'' a pet, what do you do?"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"''Check out'' em um hotel significa fazer o check-out, ou seja, pagar a conta e sair. A opção ''To arrive and get the key'' é ''check in''.","options":["To pay the bill and leave","To arrive and get the key","To look at the room","To make a reservation"],"prompt":"At a hotel, what does ''check out'' mean?"},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"''Put off'' significa adiar. A frase indica que a reunião foi adiada para amanhã, não cancelada.","options":["To cancel the meeting","To start the meeting","To delay the meeting","To finish the meeting"],"prompt":"What does ''put off'' mean in the sentence: \"We had to put off the meeting till tomorrow.\"?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"''Try on'' significa experimentar roupa, ou seja, vestir para ver se serve. A opção correta é essa.","options":["You are buying them immediately.","You are putting them on to see if they fit.","You are looking at them in the store.","You are returning them to the rack."],"prompt":"If you ''try on'' clothes, what are you doing?"},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"''Get by'' significa se virar, conseguir viver ou lidar com as coisas mesmo com dificuldades. A opção ''To manage to live or do things'' é a correta.","options":["To pass a test","To travel around","To manage to live or do things","To be understood by others"],"prompt":"What does ''get by'' mean in the sentence: \"With my basic English, I can get by in London.\"?"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
on conflict (id) do update set title=excluded.title, body=excluded.body, questions=excluded.questions, is_published=excluded.is_published, updated_at=now();

insert into public.grammar_topics (id, language, level, title, overview_pt_br, formation_pt_br, use_cases, common_mistakes, notes_pt_br, sort_order, is_published)
values ('generated-grammar-en-b1-b677ac691538', 'en', 'B1', 'Present Perfect vs. Past Simple', 'Nesta lição, você vai aprender a diferença entre o Present Perfect e o Past Simple em inglês. O Past Simple é usado para ações terminadas em um momento específico no passado, enquanto o Present Perfect conecta o passado com o presente. Vamos explorar quando usar cada um e como formar frases corretamente.', 'Past Simple: sujeito + verbo no passado (regular: -ed, irregular: forma própria). Exemplo: ''I visited'' (eu visitei). Present Perfect: sujeito + have/has + particípio passado. Exemplo: ''I have visited'' (eu visitei/algo que aconteceu mas tem relevância agora).', '["Use o Past Simple para ações concluídas em um tempo definido no passado (ex: yesterday, last week).","Use o Present Perfect para ações que aconteceram em um tempo indefinido, mas que têm relevância para o presente.","Use o Present Perfect com ''ever'', ''never'', ''already'', ''yet'', ''just'' para experiências e ações recentes.","Use o Past Simple para perguntas com ''when'' ou ''what time''."]'::jsonb, '["Usar o Present Perfect com expressões de tempo definido como ''yesterday'' ou ''in 2010''.","Esquecer de usar o particípio passado no Present Perfect (ex: ''I have went'' em vez de ''I have gone'').","Usar o Past Simple quando a ação tem relevância no presente (ex: ''I lost my keys'' quando ainda não as encontrei).","Confundir a forma do verbo no past simple e no past participle de verbos irregulares."]'::jsonb, '["No inglês britânico, o Present Perfect é mais usado para ações recentes; no americano, às vezes se usa o Past Simple.","''Since'' e ''for'' são usados com o Present Perfect para indicar duração.","O Past Simple é usado em narrativas para contar histórias de eventos concluídos.","Nunca use ''ago'' com o Present Perfect; use com o Past Simple."]'::jsonb, 0, true)
