insert into public.quick_lessons (id, language, level, title, body, question, options, answer_index, questions, sort_order, is_published)
values ('generated-quick-lesson-en-b1-5a5ecbdc3194', 'en', 'B1', 'What Would You Do? Zero, First, and Second Conditionals', 'Conditionals are sentences that talk about possible situations and their results. In English, we use different conditionals for different kinds of situations. At B1 level, you need to know three main types: zero, first, and second conditionals.

The zero conditional is for things that are always true. We use it for facts, scientific truths, and habits. The structure is: if + present simple, present simple. For example, "If you heat water to 100 degrees Celsius, it boils." Another example: "If I drink coffee at night, I don''t sleep well." This shows a general rule or a routine.

The first conditional is for real and possible situations in the future. We use it when we think something is likely to happen. The structure is: if + present simple, will + base verb. For example, "If it rains tomorrow, we will stay at home." Or "If you study hard, you will pass the exam." The condition is possible, and the result is a prediction or a plan.

The second conditional is for unreal or imaginary situations in the present or future, and also for giving advice. We use it to talk about things that are unlikely or even impossible. The structure is: if + past simple, would + base verb. For example, "If I won the lottery, I would travel around the world." Or "If I were you, I would apologize." Notice that we use "were" for all subjects in formal English, though many people say "was" in informal speech.

Now, let''s see how to choose between them. If you are talking about a fact or habit, use zero. If the situation is real and possible, use first. If the situation is imaginary or unreal, use second. For example, "If you work out regularly, you feel better" is zero. "If you work out tomorrow, you will feel better" is first. "If you worked out more, you would feel better" is second.

A common mistake is mixing the tenses. For instance, saying "If I will have time, I will come" is incorrect. In the first conditional, we don''t use "will" in the if-clause. In the second conditional, we use past simple, but it doesn''t mean past time; it means unreal present.

In summary, conditionals help us talk about different levels of possibility. Remember:
- Zero: general truths (if + present, present)
- First: real future possibilities (if + present, will)
- Second: unreal present or advice (if + past, would)

Practice these structures to express yourself more precisely in English. Now, test your understanding with the questions below.', 'Choose the correct form to complete the zero conditional sentence: "If you ______ ice, it melts."', '["will heat","heat","would heat","heated"]'::jsonb, 1, '[{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Na zero conditional, usamos o presente simples nas duas partes da frase. A opção correta é ''heat''.","options":["will heat","heat","would heat","heated"],"prompt":"Choose the correct form to complete the zero conditional sentence: \"If you ______ ice, it melts.\""},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"A primeira condicional usa ''if + presente simples'' e ''will + verbo''. A opção 2 é a única com essa estrutura.","options":["If I had more time, I would learn piano.","If you study, you will pass the exam.","If you freeze water, it becomes ice.","If I were rich, I would buy a car."],"prompt":"Which sentence is an example of the first conditional?"},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"Na segunda condicional, usamos o passado simples. Para ''I'', a forma correta é ''were'' (embora ''was'' seja usado informalmente).","options":["am","will be","were","be"],"prompt":"Complete the second conditional sentence: \"If I ______ (be) you, I would talk to her.\""},{"answer_index":3,"explanation_pt_br":"A primeira condicional correta usa ''if + presente simples'' e ''will + verbo''. A opção 4 é a única correta. As outras não seguem a estrutura.","options":["If it rains tomorrow, we stay at home.","If it rained tomorrow, we would stay at home.","If it will rain tomorrow, we will stay at home.","If it rains tomorrow, we will stay at home."],"prompt":"Choose the correct sentence with the first conditional:"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
