insert into public.quick_lessons (id, language, level, title, body, question, options, answer_index, questions, sort_order, is_published)
values ('generated-quick-lesson-en-b1-b9d2eafc4fe1', 'en', 'B1', 'Reporting What Others Say: An Introduction to Reported Speech', 'Have you ever told a friend what your teacher said? Or repeated a piece of news you heard on TV? In English, we often need to report what other people say. Doing it correctly can make you sound more fluent and accurate. Let''s explore the basics of reported speech.

First, what is reported speech? It''s when you tell someone what another person said, without using their exact words. For example, if John says, "I am tired," you can report it: "John said that he was tired." Notice that the verb tense changes. We often ''shift back'' one tense. The present ''am'' becomes the past ''was''. This is called ''backshift''. It''s a common pattern in English.

Let''s look at another example. If Maria says, "I will help you," she is speaking about the future. When you report her words, you might say, "Maria said that she would help me." Here, ''will'' changes to ''would''. This backshift is important because it makes the reported speech sound natural in English.

However, backshift isn''t the only change. Pronouns and time expressions may also change. For instance, if someone says, "I am busy today," you might report it later as, "She said that she was busy that day." ''Today'' becomes ''that day''. These little changes help keep the meaning clear for the listener.

Now, when do we use reported speech? We use it in everyday conversations, in news reports, and in stories. For example, a news anchor might say, "The mayor announced that the festival would be postponed." In a story, a narrator might say, "He told me that he was happy." Being able to use reported speech allows you to share information accurately and politely.

It''s also useful when you want to ''report'' questions or commands. For yes/no questions, we use ''if'' or ''whether''. For example, "She asked if I liked coffee." For commands, we use ''told'' or ''asked'' + infinitive. For example, "The boss told us to arrive early." These patterns are very common in professional and academic settings.

In conclusion, reported speech is a valuable tool in English. It helps you share what others say while adjusting the grammar to fit the context. As you practice, you''ll notice that it becomes more natural. Remember the key changes: tense backshift, pronoun changes, and time expressions. Now, let''s check your understanding with a quick quiz.', 'What is reported speech?', '["Using the exact words someone said.","Reporting what someone said without using their exact words.","Talking about the future.","Quoting a text from a book."]'::jsonb, 1, '[{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Discurso relatado é quando você conta o que alguém disse sem usar as palavras exatas.","options":["Using the exact words someone said.","Reporting what someone said without using their exact words.","Talking about the future.","Quoting a text from a book."],"prompt":"What is reported speech?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Normalmente, o tempo verbal recua um tempo no discurso relatado, chamado ''backshift''.","options":["It stays the same.","It shifts back one tense.","It shifts forward one tense.","It changes to future."],"prompt":"When we report a statement, what usually happens to the verb tense?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"No discurso relatado, ''am'' (presente) vira ''was'' (passado).","options":["John said that he is tired.","John said that he was tired.","John said that he is being tired.","John said that he has been tired."],"prompt":"Choose the correct reported speech for: ''I am tired,'' said John."},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Para perguntas sim/não, usamos ''if'' ou ''whether'' no discurso relatado.","options":["what","if","that","why"],"prompt":"In reported speech, what word do we use to report a yes/no question?"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
