insert into public.quick_lessons (id, language, level, title, body, question, options, answer_index, questions, sort_order, is_published)
values ('generated-quick-lesson-en-b1-bb863e4c0b03', 'en', 'B1', 'Defining and Non-Defining Relative Clauses', 'Relative clauses are a powerful tool in English. They help us give extra information about a person, thing, or idea. There are two types: defining and non-defining.

Defining relative clauses are essential. They tell us exactly which person or thing we are talking about. Without them, the sentence would be incomplete or unclear. For example: "The woman who lives next door is a doctor." Here, "who lives next door" is necessary to know which woman. We do not use commas with defining relative clauses.

Non-defining relative clauses, on the other hand, add extra information. We already know which person or thing we mean. The extra information is not essential; we could remove it without changing the basic meaning. For example: "My sister, who lives in São Paulo, is a doctor." The clause "who lives in São Paulo" adds information, but it is not necessary to identify my sister. We always use commas with non-defining relative clauses.

In conversation, we often use defining relative clauses. For example, "The book that you lent me is great." Here, "that you lent me" identifies which book. In non-defining clauses, we cannot use "that"; we must use "who" for people and "which" for things. Also, in non-defining clauses, we cannot omit the relative pronoun.

When we talk about Brazilian culture, we use these structures naturally. For instance: "Brazilians, who love football, watch many matches." Here, the non-defining clause gives extra information about all Brazilians. In contrast, "The player who scored the goal is from Rio" is defining because it specifies which player.

Another difference is in pronunciation. In defining clauses, we do not pause. In non-defining clauses, we pause, which is shown by commas in writing. This pause helps the listener understand that the information is additional.

Remember: if the clause is essential, no commas. If it''s extra, use commas. Also, use "that" only for defining clauses, and avoid "that" in non-defining clauses. These simple rules will help you sound more natural in English.

Now let''s practice with some questions to check your understanding.', 'Which sentence contains a defining relative clause?', '["My brother, who lives in Curitiba, is an engineer.","The cake that she made was delicious.","Rio de Janeiro, which is famous for its beaches, is beautiful.","My car, which is very old, still works."]'::jsonb, 1, '[{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"A oração ''that she made'' é essencial para identificar qual bolo. As outras opções têm orações não definidoras, com vírgulas, que apenas adicionam informação extra.","options":["My brother, who lives in Curitiba, is an engineer.","The cake that she made was delicious.","Rio de Janeiro, which is famous for its beaches, is beautiful.","My car, which is very old, still works."],"prompt":"Which sentence contains a defining relative clause?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Em uma oração não definidora, usamos vírgulas. A opção correta é ''My mother, who is very kind, helps everyone.'' A ausência de vírgulas na opção B a torna incorreta para o sentido pretendido.","options":["The teacher who helped me is from Portugal.","My mother who is very kind helps everyone.","The movie that we watched was boring.","The city where I was born is small."],"prompt":"Choose the sentence with the correct punctuation for a non-defining relative clause."},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Em ''The book which is on the table is mine'', ''which'' é o sujeito da oração relativa; não podemos omiti-lo. Nas opções com objeto (''who'' ou ''whom''), podemos omitir. Neste caso, a opção B é a única em que o pronome é sujeito, portanto não pode ser omitido. Mas a pergunta pede onde podemos omitir; na opção B não podemos. Na verdade, a resposta correta é a opção C, pois ''whom'' é objeto e pode ser omitido. Vamos corrigir: a opção correta é ''The woman I met is a lawyer.'' Portanto, a resposta correta é a opção 2 (índice 2) se considerarmos a opção C. Mas no array, a opção C é a terceira (índice 2). Vou ajustar.","options":["The man who lives next door is a chef.","The book which is on the table is mine.","The woman whom I met is a lawyer.","The friend who helped me is from Bahia."],"prompt":"In which sentence can we omit the relative pronoun?"},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"Em orações não definidoras, não usamos ''that''. Usamos ''who'' para pessoas, ''which'' para coisas e ''whose'' para posse. Portanto, ''that'' não é usado.","options":["who","which","that","whose"],"prompt":"Which relative pronoun is NOT used in non-defining relative clauses?"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
