insert into public.quick_lessons (id, language, level, title, body, question, options, answer_index, questions, sort_order, is_published)
values ('generated-quick-lesson-en-b1-9e21d7f446fc', 'en', 'B1', 'Deciding What to Do: Gerunds and Infinitives', 'When you talk about your plans, preferences, or feelings, you often need to use another verb after the main verb. In English, there are two common forms for this: the gerund (verb + -ing) and the infinitive (to + verb). Choosing the right form can change the meaning of your sentence, so it''s important to practice.

First, let''s look at gerunds. A gerund is the -ing form of a verb used as a noun. For example, in the sentence “I enjoy reading,” “reading” is a gerund. We use gerunds after certain verbs like “enjoy,” “mind,” “suggest,” and “avoid.” For example: “She avoids driving in heavy traffic.” We also use gerunds after prepositions: “He is interested in learning Spanish.”

Now, let''s look at infinitives. An infinitive is “to” + the base verb, like “to read.” We use infinitives after verbs like “want,” “need,” “decide,” and “hope.” For example: “They decided to move to a new city.” We also use infinitives after adjectives: “It''s important to arrive on time.”

Sometimes, both forms are correct, but the meaning changes. For example, “I remember locking the door” means you have a memory of doing it. “I remembered to lock the door” means you didn''t forget to do it. “Stop smoking” means quit the habit, while “stop to smoke” means pause in order to smoke. These differences are important for clear communication.

Another tricky point is when verbs are followed by a direct object and then an infinitive. For example: “She told me to wait.” Here, “me” is the object, and “to wait” is the infinitive. We use this structure with verbs like “tell,” “ask,” “advise,” and “encourage.” For example: “They advised us to buy travel insurance.”

Finally, remember that some verbs are followed by a gerund, and some by an infinitive, and there''s no simple rule. You need to memorize them. For example, “consider” takes a gerund: “I''m considering changing jobs.” “Offer” takes an infinitive: “He offered to help.” With practice, you''ll start to feel what''s right.

To sum up, using gerunds and infinitives correctly helps you express yourself more precisely. Pay attention to the main verb and the meaning you want to convey. In the questions that follow, test your understanding of these patterns.', 'Which sentence uses a gerund correctly?', '["He suggested to go to the beach.","They enjoy to play soccer on weekends.","She avoids eating fast food.","We finished to do our homework."]'::jsonb, 2, '[{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"O verbo ''avoid'' é seguido de gerúndio, então ''eating'' está correto. As outras opções usam infinitivo incorretamente após verbos que exigem gerúndio.","options":["He suggested to go to the beach.","They enjoy to play soccer on weekends.","She avoids eating fast food.","We finished to do our homework."],"prompt":"Which sentence uses a gerund correctly?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"''Decide'' é seguido de infinitivo, então ''to stay'' está certo. As outras opções usam formas incorretas.","options":["I want going home now.","She decided to stay for dinner.","He enjoys to read novels.","They suggested to meet later."],"prompt":"Choose the correct sentence with an infinitive."},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"A primeira frase indica uma lembrança de uma ação passada, enquanto a segunda indica que você não esqueceu de fazer a ação.","options":["No difference; both mean the same thing.","The first means you have a memory of locking it; the second means you didn''t forget to do it.","The first means you will lock it later; the second means you locked it in the past.","The first is incorrect; only the second is correct."],"prompt":"What is the difference between ''I remember locking the door'' and ''I remembered to lock the door''?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Aqui, ''stop to buy'' significa parar a fim de comprar café. As outras opções têm significados diferentes ou uso incorreto.","options":["She stopped to smoke last year.","He stopped to buy coffee on his way to work.","They stopped to watch TV for hours.","We stopped to eat at that restaurant yesterday."],"prompt":"Which sentence correctly uses ''stop''?"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
