insert into public.quick_lessons (id, language, level, title, body, question, options, answer_index, questions, sort_order, is_published)
values ('generated-quick-lesson-en-b1-579da79e34b4', 'en', 'B1', 'Everyday Phrasal Verbs for Work and Home', 'Phrasal verbs are a big part of everyday English. They are usually made of a verb plus a small word like ''up'', ''out'', or ''off''. For example, you might ''wake up'', ''turn on'' the TV, or ''pick up'' your phone. They are very common in conversations, so learning them helps you understand and speak more naturally.

Let''s look at a few common ones you can use at work. ''Call off'' means to cancel something. If a meeting is cancelled, your boss might say, "Let''s call off the meeting." ''Put off'' means to delay something. For example, "We''ll put off the decision until next week." ''Carry out'' means to do or complete a task. The police might ''carry out'' an investigation. And ''figure out'' means to understand or solve a problem. You might say, "I can''t figure out this math exercise."

At home, you use phrasal verbs all the time too. ''Pick up'' can mean to lift something or to collect someone. You can ''pick up'' your clothes from the floor, or ''pick up'' your friend at the airport. ''Throw away'' means to discard something, like old newspapers. ''Turn on'' and ''turn off'' are used for devices. You can ''turn on'' the lights, or ''turn off'' the TV. ''Look for'' means to search for something. If you can''t find your keys, you ''look for'' them.

Some phrasal verbs are more formal than others. In business writing, you might ''carry out'' a plan, but in conversation, you would ''do'' it. ''Call off'' is neutral – you can use it in most situations. ''Put off'' is also common, but you need to be careful – in informal English, you can also say ''procrastinate'', but that''s a more formal word.

Learning phrasal verbs is like learning new vocabulary. You need to see them in context and practice. One way is to write sentences with them. For example, "I need to pick up my dry cleaning today." Or, "She called off the picnic because of the rain." Another way is to listen for them in songs or movies. Many songs use phrasal verbs because they sound natural and rhythmic.

In the next part, we will practice with some questions. Remember, don''t be afraid to make mistakes – that''s how we learn. Phrasal verbs are essential for fluency, and with practice, you''ll start using them confidently. Now, let''s try some exercises to see how well you understand them.', 'What does ''call off'' mean?', '["To cancel","To start","To delay","To finish"]'::jsonb, 0, '[{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"''Call off'' significa cancelar algo. Por exemplo, cancelar uma reunião.","options":["To cancel","To start","To delay","To finish"],"prompt":"What does ''call off'' mean?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"''Put off'' significa adiar ou adiar algo.","options":["Carry out","Put off","Pick up","Turn on"],"prompt":"Which phrasal verb means ''to delay''?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"''Carry out'' significa executar ou realizar uma tarefa.","options":["To lift","To complete","To discard","To search"],"prompt":"What is the meaning of ''carry out''?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"''Pick up'' pode significar buscar alguém, como no aeroporto.","options":["Call them","Collect them","Delay them","Turn them off"],"prompt":"If you ''pick up'' a friend at the airport, you..."}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
