insert into public.quick_lessons (id, language, level, title, body, question, options, answer_index, questions, sort_order, is_published)
values ('generated-quick-lesson-en-b1-0f3da7f9793f', 'en', 'B1', 'Connecting Stories: Using Narrative Discourse Markers', 'When you tell a story in English, it''s not just about the words you use, but also about how you connect them. Narrative discourse markers are words and phrases that help your listener or reader understand the order of events, the relationships between ideas, and your attitude towards the story. They make your narrative flow smoothly and keep your audience engaged. Without them, your story can sound like a list of separate events. In this lesson, you will learn and practice some common discourse markers that will make your English stories more natural and effective.

First, let''s look at sequencing markers. These show the order in which things happen. Words like ''first'', ''then'', ''next'', ''after that'', and ''finally'' are your best friends when you want to tell a story in a clear way. For example, you might say, ''First, I woke up early. Then, I had a big breakfast. After that, I went for a walk. Finally, I met my friends for lunch.'' Notice how these markers guide us through the story, step by step.

Another important group is cause and effect markers. These show why something happened. Words like ''so'', ''therefore'', ''because of this'', and ''as a result'' help you explain the reasons behind events. For instance, ''The weather was terrible. Therefore, we stayed inside all day.'' Or, ''I forgot my umbrella. So, I got completely wet.'' These markers make it clear that one event led to another.

Contrast markers are also useful. They show a difference or an opposite idea. Words like ''however'', ''but'', ''although'', and ''on the other hand'' can make your story more interesting by adding unexpected turns. For example, ''I usually love spicy food. However, this dish was too hot for me.'' Or, ''She was tired. But, she decided to go for a run anyway.'' These markers create a sense of surprise or conflict in your narrative.

Finally, there are emphasizing and clarifying markers. These help you highlight important points or explain something better. Words like ''in fact'', ''actually'', ''especially'', and ''for example'' are great for adding details. For example, ''Everyone enjoyed the party. In fact, my grandmother danced all night!'' Or, ''I love animals. Actually, I have three cats and a dog.'' These markers make your story more vivid and personal.

Now, let''s practice! Read the following questions and choose the best marker to complete each sentence. Remember to think about the meaning of the sentence and which marker fits best based on the context.

As you practice, you will become more comfortable with these markers. Try to use them in your own stories. When you speak or write, pay attention to how you connect your ideas. With time and practice, using narrative discourse markers will become second nature. You will be telling engaging and coherent stories in English in no time!', 'Which discourse marker best completes the sentence: ''I was very hungry. ______, I ate two sandwiches.''', '["However","Therefore","First","On the other hand"]'::jsonb, 1, '[{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"''Therefore'' indica causa e efeito: a fome foi a causa de comer dois sanduíches. ''However'' e ''On the other hand'' são contrastes, e ''First'' é sequência.","options":["However","Therefore","First","On the other hand"],"prompt":"Which discourse marker best completes the sentence: ''I was very hungry. ______, I ate two sandwiches.''"},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"''First'' é usado para introduzir a primeira ação em uma sequência. ''Finally'' indica a última, e os outros são de contraste ou causa.","options":["Finally","However","First","Therefore"],"prompt":"Which marker shows the correct sequence for a story: ''______, I brushed my teeth. Then, I got dressed.''"},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"''However'' é usado para contrastar ideias: apesar de longo, foi bom. ''So'' e ''Because of this'' indicam causa, e ''After that'' é sequência.","options":["So","Because of this","However","After that"],"prompt":"Choose the best marker to show a contrast: ''The movie was very long. ______, it was really good.''"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"''In fact'' é usado para enfatizar e adicionar informação que reforça o que foi dito. ''First'' é sequência, ''Therefore'' é consequência, e ''But'' é contraste.","options":["In fact","First","Therefore","But"],"prompt":"Which marker emphasizes an additional detail: ''My brother is very talented. ______, he can play the guitar and the piano.''"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
