insert into public.quick_lessons (id, language, level, title, body, question, options, answer_index, questions, sort_order, is_published)
values ('generated-quick-lesson-en-b1-ad67a7d748ce', 'en', 'B1', 'Present Perfect vs. Past Simple: Telling Stories About Your Life', 'Have you ever told a story about something that happened to you? In English, you have two common ways to talk about the past: the past simple and the present perfect. Understanding the difference is important for clear communication. In this lesson, you will learn how to use both tenses correctly in everyday conversations.

First, let''s look at the past simple. We use it for actions that started and finished at a specific time in the past. For example, you can say, "I visited my grandmother last weekend." The time is clear: last weekend. You know exactly when it happened. Other time expressions like yesterday, in 2010, or two days ago also go with the past simple. You form the past simple by adding -ed to regular verbs, but many common verbs are irregular, like go-went, see-saw, or eat-ate.

Now, let''s talk about the present perfect. We use it for actions that happened at an unspecified time in the past, or that started in the past and continue to the present. For example, "I have visited my grandmother many times." Here, you don''t say when. The present perfect connects the past to the present. You form it with have/has + past participle. Regular past participles end in -ed, but irregular verbs have special forms, like gone, seen, or eaten.

One common mistake is using the past simple with time expressions like ''already'', ''just'', or ''yet'', because those usually require the present perfect. For example, you should say, "I have just eaten," not "I just ate" (though in American English, the past simple is sometimes used informally, but for exams, use the present perfect). Another mistake is forgetting that ''for'' and ''since'' often go with the present perfect, as in "I have lived here for five years" or "I have known her since 2010."

Let''s practice with a story. Imagine you are talking about your life experiences. You might say, "I have traveled to many countries." That''s a general experience. But if you tell a specific story about your trip to Italy last year, you use the past simple: "I went to Italy in 2023." The choice depends on whether you focus on the result or the action time.

To summarize: Use past simple for finished actions with a specific time. Use present perfect for experiences, changes, or actions that affect the present. Pay attention to time words to help you choose. Now you are ready to practice!
', 'Which sentence uses the past simple correctly?', '["I have visited my grandmother last weekend.","I visited my grandmother last weekend.","I have visited my grandmother.","I visit my grandmother last weekend."]'::jsonb, 1, '[{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"\"last weekend\" indica um tempo específico no passado, então usamos o past simple: ''visited''.","options":["I have visited my grandmother last weekend.","I visited my grandmother last weekend.","I have visited my grandmother.","I visit my grandmother last weekend."],"prompt":"Which sentence uses the past simple correctly?"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"O present perfect é formado com ''has/have + particípio''. Para ''she'', usamos ''has been'', indicando experiência sem tempo definido.","options":["has been","was","is","have been"],"prompt":"Choose the correct present perfect form: ''She ___ to Brazil many times.''"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"O uso de ''now'' mostra conexão com o presente. ''have finished'' é o present perfect, indicando que a ação terminou e tem relevância agora.","options":["finished","have finished","finish","was finishing"],"prompt":"Complete the sentence with the correct tense: ''I ___ (finish) my homework. Now I can go out.''"},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"''yesterday'' é um tempo específico, então deve usar past simple: ''I saw that movie yesterday''. O present perfect não é usado com ''yesterday''.","options":["She has lost her keys.","He has worked here for two years.","I have seen that movie yesterday.","They have lived in São Paulo since 2015."],"prompt":"Which sentence is incorrect?"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
