values ('generated-quick-lesson-en-b1-592a90cca4af', 'en', 'B1', 'If You Try, You''ll Learn: Zero, First, and Second Conditionals', 'Conditional sentences are everywhere in English. They help us talk about facts, possibilities, and imaginary situations. For Brazilian learners at B1 level, understanding the zero, first, and second conditionals is a big step. Let''s explore each one.

The zero conditional is for things that are always true. We use it for scientific facts, rules, and habits. For example: "If you heat water to 100°C, it boils." The structure is simple: if + present simple, present simple. We can also use "when" instead of "if" because these things always happen.

The first conditional is for real and possible situations in the future. It shows a cause and effect. For example: "If it rains tomorrow, we will cancel the picnic." Here, we use if + present simple, will + base verb. This conditional is common in everyday conversations when we make plans or promises.

The second conditional is for unreal or imaginary situations in the present or future. It helps us dream or give advice. For example: "If I had a million dollars, I would travel the world." The structure is if + past simple, would + base verb. We use it when the situation is not true now.

A common mistake is confusing the first and second conditionals. Remember, the first is for possible things, the second for unlikely or imaginary ones. For instance, "If I have time, I will call you" (possible) versus "If I had time, I would call you" (but I don''t have time now).

Practice is key. Try making your own examples. Think about facts, future plans, and dreams. With time, you''ll use these naturally. Now, let''s test your understanding with some questions!', 'Which sentence is an example of the zero conditional?', '["If I study, I will pass.","If you freeze water, it becomes ice.","If I were rich, I would buy a car.","If she comes, we will leave."]'::jsonb, 1, '[{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"A zero conditional expressa fatos gerais ou verdades científicas: se você congela água, ela vira gelo. As outras opções são primeira ou segunda condicional.","options":["If I study, I will pass.","If you freeze water, it becomes ice.","If I were rich, I would buy a car.","If she comes, we will leave."],"prompt":"Which sentence is an example of the zero conditional?"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"Na primeira condicional, usamos ''if + presente simples'' e ''will + verbo''. A opção correta é ''If I see him, I will tell him''.","options":["If I see him, I will tell him.","If I saw him, I would tell him.","If I see him, I would tell him.","If I saw him, I will tell him."],"prompt":"Choose the correct first conditional sentence:"},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"A segunda condicional expressa situações imaginárias ou irreais. ''If I were you'' é uma situação hipotética, por isso a resposta correta.","options":["If you mix red and white, you get pink.","If I have time, I will help you.","If I were you, I would apologize.","If she studies, she will pass."],"prompt":"Which sentence expresses an imaginary situation?"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"Na primeira condicional, usamos o presente simples na oração ''if''. Portanto, ''if he arrives''.","options":["arrives","arrived","would arrive","will arrive"],"prompt":"Fill in the blank: If he ______ (arrive) early, we''ll start the meeting at 9."}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
