insert into public.quick_lessons (id, language, level, title, body, question, options, answer_index, questions, sort_order, is_published)
values ('generated-quick-lesson-en-b1-c254fd575068', 'en', 'B1', 'Reporting What People Say: An Introduction to Reported Speech', 'Welcome to this lesson on reported speech! In English, we often need to tell someone what another person said. Instead of repeating their exact words, we can use reported speech. This is very useful in conversations, at work, or when telling stories. Let''s learn how to do it correctly.

First, think about the difference between direct speech and reported speech. Direct speech is when we use the exact words of the speaker, often with quotation marks. For example, Maria says, "I am tired." Reported speech is when we talk about what someone said without using their exact words. For example, Maria says that she is tired. Notice that we often change the pronoun and the verb tense.

Now, let''s look at the main changes in reported speech. When we report a statement in the present tense, we usually shift the verb one step back in time. For example, "I am happy" becomes "He said he was happy." The present simple ''am'' changes to past simple ''was.'' Similarly, "I work in São Paulo" becomes "She said she worked in São Paulo." This rule is called backshift.

What about questions? When we report questions, we use ''if'' or ''whether'' for yes/no questions. For example, "Are you hungry?" becomes "He asked if I was hungry." For wh-questions, we keep the question word: "Where is the station?" becomes "She asked where the station was." Remember to change the word order to statement order and adjust the tense.

Also, we need to think about time and place expressions. Words like ''now'' become ''then,'' ''today'' becomes ''that day,'' and ''here'' becomes ''there.'' For example, "I will come here tomorrow" becomes "He said he would go there the next day." These small changes make your English sound natural.

Common mistakes include forgetting to change the pronoun or the tense. For example, saying "He said he is tired" instead of "He said he was tired" is a mistake. Also, don''t forget to change the verb ''will'' to ''would'' and ''can'' to ''could.'' Practice these changes to improve your accuracy.

Finally, remember that reported speech is used in many situations, such as reporting news, sharing gossip, or telling a story. It helps you sound more fluent and precise. Listen to native speakers and notice how they report what others say. Now, let''s test your understanding with some questions.

Are you ready? Here are the questions to check what you learned.', 'Choose the sentence that correctly changes direct speech to reported speech: "I like coffee," said Ana.', '["Ana said that she likes coffee.","Ana said that she liked coffee.","Ana said that I like coffee.","Ana said that she liked coffees."]'::jsonb, 1, '[{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Na reported speech, geralmente mudamos o tempo verbal para o passado. ''Like'' vira ''liked''. Além disso, mudamos o pronome ''I'' para ''she'' para refletir a pessoa que falou. Portanto, a opção correta é ''Ana said that she liked coffee.''","options":["Ana said that she likes coffee.","Ana said that she liked coffee.","Ana said that I like coffee.","Ana said that she liked coffees."],"prompt":"Choose the sentence that correctly changes direct speech to reported speech: \"I like coffee,\" said Ana."},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"Quando usamos reported speech, ''will'' muda para ''would'', e ''tomorrow'' muda para ''the next day''. Portanto, a frase correta é ''He said that he would call me the next day.''","options":["He said that he will call me tomorrow.","He said that he would call me tomorrow.","He said that he would call me the next day.","He said that he will call me the next day."],"prompt":"Which is the correct reported speech for: \"I will call you tomorrow,\" he said."},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"Para perguntas sim/não, usamos ''if''. O tempo verbal muda de presente contínuo (are studying) para passado contínuo (was studying). ''Now'' vira ''then''. Assim, a resposta correta é ''She asked if I was studying then.''","options":["She asked if I was studying then.","She asked if I am studying now.","She asked if I was studying now.","She asked if I were studying then."],"prompt":"Report the question: \"Are you studying now?\" she asked."},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Na reported speech, ''can'' vira ''could''. Também mantemos a estrutura ''help'' sem passar para o passado. Portanto, a opção correta é ''She told me that she could help me.''","options":["She told me that she can help me.","She told me that she could help me.","She told me that she could helped me.","She told me that she can helped me."],"prompt":"Choose the correct reported speech: \"I can help you,\" she told me."}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
