insert into public.quick_lessons (id, language, level, title, body, question, options, answer_index, questions, sort_order, is_published)
values ('generated-quick-lesson-en-b1-fe39dab94651', 'en', 'B1', 'Modal Verbs: Deduction, Obligation, and Advice', 'Modal verbs are small but powerful words in English. They help us express ideas like possibility, necessity, and advice. In this lesson, we will focus on three main uses: deduction, obligation, and advice.

First, let''s talk about deduction. When we are not sure about something, we can use modal verbs to make a guess. For example, if a friend looks tired, you might say, "You must be tired." Here, ''must'' shows that you are almost certain because of the evidence. If you are less sure, you can use ''might'' or ''could'': "She might be at home now." These modals help us talk about the present and the past. For the past, we use ''must have'' or ''might have'': "He must have forgotten the meeting."

Second, we use modals to talk about obligation. ''Must'' and ''have to'' express strong necessity. For example, "You must wear a seatbelt" or "I have to finish this report." However, ''must'' often comes from the speaker''s opinion, while ''have to'' usually comes from an external rule. Also, ''must not'' means it is forbidden, while ''don''t have to'' means it is not necessary. For example, "You must not smoke here" and "You don''t have to come early."

Third, modals are used for advice. ''Should'' and ''ought to'' are common. For example, "You should eat more vegetables" or "You ought to see a doctor." These are softer than ''must'' but still give a recommendation. We can also use ''had better'' for strong advice, often with a warning: "You''d better leave now or you''ll miss the bus."

Now, let''s see these in context. Imagine you are planning a trip. You might say, "We must book the hotel soon because prices could go up." Or if a colleague looks stressed, you could advise, "You should take a break." These modals make your English more natural and precise.

Remember, modals are followed by the base form of the verb (without ''to''). Also, they don''t change form with he/she/it. For example, "He must go" not "He musts go." Practice using them in different situations.

Now let''s check your understanding with some questions.', 'Which sentence expresses a deduction about the past?', '["She must finish her homework.","He might have missed the train.","You should call him.","I have to go now."]'::jsonb, 1, '[{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"A frase ''He might have missed the train'' usa ''might have'' + particípio passado para expressar uma dedução sobre o passado, ou seja, uma suposição sobre algo que pode ter acontecido.","options":["She must finish her homework.","He might have missed the train.","You should call him.","I have to go now."],"prompt":"Which sentence expresses a deduction about the past?"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"''You must be quiet in the library'' indica uma obrigação imposta por uma regra externa, pois a biblioteca exige silêncio. ''Must'' expressa obrigação forte, e aqui é claramente uma regra.","options":["You must be quiet in the library.","You must be tired.","You might like this book.","You should try this cake."],"prompt":"Which sentence expresses a strong obligation from an external rule?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"''Don''t have to'' significa ''não é necessário'', ou seja, não há obrigação de pagar. Diferente de ''must not'', que proíbe.","options":["It is forbidden to pay.","It is not necessary to pay.","You must pay.","You should pay."],"prompt":"What does ''You don''t have to pay'' mean?"},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"Após modais como ''must'', ''should'', ''can'', usamos o verbo na forma base sem ''to''. A única opção correta é ''They have to work'', porque ''have to'' é diferente e usa ''to''.","options":["He must to study.","She should to go.","They have to work.","I can to help."],"prompt":"Which sentence is correct?"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
