insert into public.quick_lessons (id, language, level, title, body, question, options, answer_index, questions, sort_order, is_published)
values ('generated-quick-lesson-en-b1-09b1d34ac8d6', 'en', 'B1', 'Present Perfect vs Past Simple', 'Have you ever wondered when to use ''I have seen'' and when to say ''I saw''? For many Brazilian learners, this is a common challenge. In this lesson, we will clarify the differences between the present perfect and the past simple. Understanding these tenses will help you speak more naturally and precisely about your experiences and past actions.

First, let''s talk about the past simple. We use it to talk about actions that started and finished at a specific time in the past. For example, ''I visited my grandmother last weekend.'' Here, the time is clear: last weekend. The past simple is also used for a sequence of events in a story, like ''I woke up, had breakfast, and left.'' Regular verbs in the past simple end in ''-ed'', but there are many irregular verbs, such as ''go'' -> ''went'' and ''see'' -> ''saw''.

Now, the present perfect is formed with ''have'' or ''has'' plus the past participle of the verb. For example, ''I have visited my grandmother.'' We use it when the action happened at some indefinite time before now, or when the action has a connection to the present. For instance, ''I have lost my keys'' means I don''t have them now. We also use it to talk about experiences, like ''Have you ever eaten feijoada?'' or to talk about actions that started in the past and continue to the present, like ''I have lived in this city for five years'' (and I still live here).

A common mistake is using the present perfect with specific past times. For example, saying ''I have seen him yesterday'' is incorrect. We should say ''I saw him yesterday'' because ''yesterday'' is a definite time. Another mistake is using the past simple when no time is mentioned and the action has a present result. For instance, saying ''I lost my keys'' instead of ''I have lost my keys'' if you still can''t find them.

Remember, the past simple is for finished actions at a specific time, while the present perfect connects the past to the present. Practice by thinking about your own experiences. For example, ''I have traveled to Europe'' (experience) versus ''I traveled to Europe in 2019'' (specific time).

Now, let''s check your understanding with some questions. These will help you see the difference clearly. Take your time and choose the best option for each situation. After the questions, I''ll give you feedback and explanations in Portuguese. Good luck!', 'Choose the correct sentence.', '["I have visited my grandmother last weekend.","I visited my grandmother last weekend.","I have visit my grandmother last weekend.","I did visited my grandmother last weekend."]'::jsonb, 1, '[{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"A frase correta é ''I visited my grandmother last weekend'' porque ''last weekend'' indica um tempo específico no passado, então devemos usar o past simple.","options":["I have visited my grandmother last weekend.","I visited my grandmother last weekend.","I have visit my grandmother last weekend.","I did visited my grandmother last weekend."],"prompt":"Choose the correct sentence."},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"O present perfect é usado para ações que começaram no passado e continuam até o presente. ''Since 2015'' indica isso, e a forma correta é ''has lived''.","options":["She has lived in São Paulo since 2015.","She lived in São Paulo since 2015.","She has live in São Paulo since 2015.","She has living in São Paulo since 2015."],"prompt":"Which sentence uses the present perfect correctly?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Com ''yesterday'', um tempo específico, devemos usar o past simple. Portanto, ''I saw that movie yesterday'' é correto.","options":["I have seen that movie yesterday.","I saw that movie yesterday.","I have saw that movie yesterday.","I seen that movie yesterday."],"prompt":"Choose the correct sentence."},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Em perguntas sobre experiências, usamos o present perfect: ''Have you ever gone to Bahia?'' A forma é ''have + particípio''.","options":["Did / go","Have / gone","Have / went","Did / been"],"prompt":"Complete: ''_______ you ever _______ to Bahia?''"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
