insert into public.quick_lessons (id, language, level, title, body, question, options, answer_index, questions, sort_order, is_published)
values ('generated-quick-lesson-en-b1-4d9d2cbaa024', 'en', 'B1', 'Reported Speech: What People Said', 'Have you ever told a friend what another person said? Imagine your boss says, "The meeting is at 3 p.m." Later, you tell a colleague, "The boss said the meeting was at 3 p.m." This is reported speech. We use it every day to share information. In English, when we report what someone said, we often change the tense and the pronouns. This can be tricky, but with practice, it becomes easy.

In reported speech, we don''t use the exact words. Instead, we use our own words to tell the meaning. For example, if Maria says, "I am happy," you can report, "Maria said she was happy." Notice that the verb "am" changes to "was." This is called backshift. When the reporting verb is in the past (like "said"), we usually move the tense back one step.

Let''s look at more examples. If someone says, "I will call you," in reported speech it becomes, "She said she would call me." The future "will" changes to "would." Also, the pronoun "you" changes to "me" because the listener is different. Changing pronouns is important to make sense.

What about questions? If someone asks, "Do you like pizza?" we report, "He asked if I liked pizza." Here, we use "if" or "whether" for yes/no questions. The word order changes too. Instead of "do you like," we say "I liked." For questions with question words, like "Where is the station?" we say, "She asked where the station was."

Now, let''s think about the verb tense changes. Present simple becomes past simple. For example, "I work" becomes "she worked." Present continuous becomes past continuous: "I am working" becomes "she was working." Past simple often becomes past perfect: "I worked" becomes "she had worked." But not all tenses change. If the reporting verb is in the present (like "says"), we don''t change the tense. For instance, "She says, ''I am tired''" becomes "She says she is tired."

Sometimes, we don''t need to change the tense if the situation is still true. For example, if someone says, "The sun rises in the east," you can say, "He said the sun rises in the east." This is because it''s a fact. Also, time expressions change. For example, "today" becomes "that day," "tomorrow" becomes "the next day," and "yesterday" becomes "the day before."

Learning reported speech helps you communicate more clearly, especially when sharing news or stories. It''s a key skill for B1 level. Practice by listening to conversations and reporting what people say. You''ll get better with time. Now, let''s test what you''ve learned!', 'If someone says, "I am working," how do you report it?', '["He said he is working.","He said he was working.","He said he works.","He said he worked."]'::jsonb, 1, '[{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Quando o verbo de relato está no passado (said), o presente contínuo (am working) muda para passado contínuo (was working).","options":["He said he is working.","He said he was working.","He said he works.","He said he worked."],"prompt":"If someone says, \"I am working,\" how do you report it?"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"Em perguntas de sim/não em discurso indireto, usamos ''if'' ou ''whether'' e invertemos a ordem: o sujeito vem antes do verbo. ''Do you like'' vira ''I liked''.","options":["He asked if I liked coffee.","He asked do you like coffee.","He asked if do I like coffee.","He asked if I like coffee."],"prompt":"How do you report the question \"Do you like coffee?\"?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Com ''said'', o ''will'' vira ''would'', e ''tomorrow'' vira ''the next day''.","options":["She said she will travel tomorrow.","She said she would travel the next day.","She said she would travel tomorrow.","She said she travels the next day."],"prompt":"Select the correct reported speech for \"I will travel tomorrow.\""},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"Em perguntas com palavra interrogativa, mantemos a palavra, mas invertemos a ordem para sujeito + verbo, e o verbo fica no passado: ''where the bank was''.","options":["She asked where is the bank.","She asked where was the bank.","She asked where the bank was.","She asked where the bank is."],"prompt":"What is the reported speech for \"Where is the bank?\"?"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
