insert into public.quick_lessons (id, language, level, title, body, question, options, answer_index, questions, sort_order, is_published)
values ('generated-quick-lesson-en-b1-263248706410', 'en', 'B1', 'Relative Clauses: Defining and Non-Defining', 'Relative clauses are a powerful tool in English. They allow us to give extra information about a person or thing without starting a new sentence. For example, instead of saying, "I have a friend. She lives in São Paulo," we can say, "I have a friend who lives in São Paulo." The clause "who lives in São Paulo" is a relative clause. It modifies the noun "friend."

There are two types of relative clauses: defining and non-defining. Defining relative clauses are essential to the meaning of the sentence. They tell us exactly which person or thing we are talking about. For instance, "The students who study hard will pass the exam." Here, the clause "who study hard" tells us which students. Without it, the sentence would be unclear. We do not use commas with defining relative clauses.

Non-defining relative clauses, on the other hand, give extra, non-essential information. They are separated by commas. For example, "My brother, who lives in Rio, is a doctor." The clause "who lives in Rio" provides additional information about my brother, but it is not necessary to identify him. If we remove it, we still know who we are talking about: my brother.

A common mistake is confusing these two types. Many learners use commas when they are not needed, or omit them when they are. For example, writing "My sister who lives in New York is an engineer" could mean that I have more than one sister, and I am specifying which one. If I have only one sister, I should write "My sister, who lives in New York, is an engineer."

Another mistake is using ''that'' in non-defining clauses. ''That'' is only used for defining clauses. For example, we can say "The book that I bought is interesting" (defining), but we cannot say "My car, that is red, is fast". We must use ''which'' (for things) or ''who'' (for people) in non-defining clauses.

To choose the correct relative pronoun, remember: ''who'' for people, ''which'' for things, and ''that'' for both, but only in defining clauses. In non-defining clauses, always use ''who'' or ''which'', and never ''that''. Also, in non-defining clauses, you can sometimes use ''whose'' to show possession, as in "Maria, whose father is a teacher, loves school."

By mastering these rules, you will make your English more precise and natural. Practice by identifying relative clauses in texts you read, and try to create your own examples.', 'Which sentence contains a defining relative clause?', '["My cousin, who lives in Curitiba, is visiting us.","The woman who helped me was very kind.","My car, which is old, still works well.","Brazil, which is a large country, has many cultures."]'::jsonb, 1, '[{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"A oração relativa restritiva é essencial para identificar a mulher: ''who helped me''. As outras opções usam vírgulas, indicando orações não restritivas.","options":["My cousin, who lives in Curitiba, is visiting us.","The woman who helped me was very kind.","My car, which is old, still works well.","Brazil, which is a large country, has many cultures."],"prompt":"Which sentence contains a defining relative clause?"},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"A opção correta usa vírgulas para separar a informação extra sobre o chefe. As outras não têm vírgulas e são restritivas.","options":["The book that I read last week was great.","The students who passed the test were happy.","My boss, who is from Portugal, speaks three languages.","The house that they bought is expensive."],"prompt":"Which sentence correctly uses a non-defining relative clause?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"''Which'' é usado para coisas. ''Movie'' é uma coisa, e a oração é restritiva, então ''that'' também seria possível, mas ''which'' é a opção correta aqui.","options":["who","which","whose","where"],"prompt":"Choose the correct relative pronoun to complete: \"The movie ___ we saw yesterday was boring.\""},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"A opção correta usa vírgulas para separar a informação extra sobre o professor. As outras opções têm erros: falta de vírgula ou uso incorreto de ''that''.","options":["My father who is a doctor works at the hospital.","The cake, that she made, was delicious.","The teacher, who is very patient, explains well.","People who exercise are healthier."],"prompt":"Identify the option with the correct punctuation for a non-defining relative clause."}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
