insert into public.quick_lessons (id, language, level, title, body, question, options, answer_index, questions, sort_order, is_published)
values ('generated-quick-lesson-en-b1-7cb46f4c87ab', 'en', 'B1', 'Verb Patterns: Gerund or Infinitive?', 'In English, when we use two verbs together, the second verb can be in the gerund form (-ing) or the infinitive form (to + verb). For example, we say "I enjoy playing soccer" but "I want to play soccer". This can be confusing because there is no simple rule. You need to memorize which verbs are followed by which form.

Some verbs are only followed by the gerund. These include: enjoy, finish, mind, suggest, and consider. For example: "She suggested going to the beach." We also use the gerund after prepositions, like "interested in" or "good at". For example: "He is good at cooking."

Other verbs are only followed by the infinitive. These include: want, need, hope, plan, decide, and agree. For example: "They hope to travel next year." Also, we use the infinitive after adjectives, like "happy to help" or "easy to use".

Some verbs can be followed by either form, but the meaning changes. For example, "remember to do" means remember an obligation, while "remember doing" means remember a past action. "Stop to smoke" means stop in order to smoke, but "stop smoking" means quit the habit.

A good strategy is to learn the verb patterns in groups. For example, verbs about liking and disliking (like, love, hate, prefer) can be followed by gerund or infinitive, but the gerund is more common in British English. Verbs about plans and decisions (like decide, plan, intend) are usually followed by the infinitive.

To practice, try to create sentences with new verbs every day. Use a dictionary or app to check if the verb takes gerund or infinitive. Over time, you will remember the patterns naturally. Also, listen to how native speakers use these patterns in movies, songs, and podcasts.

Remember: the gerund acts like a noun, so you can say "Swimming is fun" or "I love swimming". The infinitive often expresses purpose or intention, like "I need to study". By paying attention to these patterns, you will improve your English fluency.

Now, let''s test your understanding with some questions. Read each sentence and choose the correct form of the verb in parentheses.', 'I enjoy ______ new people.', '["meet","to meet","meeting","met"]'::jsonb, 2, '[{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"Após o verbo ''enjoy'', usamos o gerúndio (-ing). Portanto, ''meeting'' é a forma correta.","options":["meet","to meet","meeting","met"],"prompt":"I enjoy ______ new people."},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Após o verbo ''decide'', usamos o infinitivo com ''to''. Portanto, ''to find'' é correto.","options":["finding","to find","find","found"],"prompt":"She decided ______ a new job."},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"Após a preposição ''in'', usamos o gerúndio. Portanto, ''learning'' é correto.","options":["learn","to learn","learning","learned"],"prompt":"They are interested in ______ English."},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"''Remember to do'' significa lembrar de uma obrigação. Portanto, ''to lock'' é correto.","options":["locking","to lock","lock","locked"],"prompt":"Please remember ______ the door when you leave."}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
