insert into public.quick_lessons (id, language, level, title, body, question, options, answer_index, questions, sort_order, is_published)
values ('generated-quick-lesson-en-b1-d9be3b884caf', 'en', 'B1', 'Present Perfect vs. Past Simple: Making the Right Choice', 'Have you ever wondered when to use “I have seen” and when to say “I saw”? Many English learners find this confusing, but don''t worry! In this lesson, we''ll look at the main differences between the present perfect and the past simple. By the end, you''ll feel more confident choosing the right tense.

First, let''s talk about the past simple. We use it for actions that started and finished in the past. The time is usually stated or understood. For example: “I visited my grandparents last weekend.” The action is complete, and the time is clear. Other examples: “She bought a new car yesterday.” “They watched a movie last night.” When we ask questions or make negative sentences, we use the auxiliary “did” plus the base verb, like “Did you eat breakfast?” or “I didn''t go to the party.”

Now, let''s look at the present perfect. We form it with “have” or “has” plus the past participle of the verb. For example: “I have finished my homework.” The present perfect connects the past to the present. We use it for something that happened at an unspecified time before now, or for an action that started in the past and continues to the present. For instance: “She has lived in São Paulo for five years.” This means she started living there five years ago and still lives there. Also: “Have you ever tried feijoada?” Here, we are asking about a life experience, not a specific time.

One of the hardest parts is knowing which tense to use. Here''s a simple rule: if you have a specific past time, like “yesterday”, “last week”, or “in 2010”, use the past simple. If the time is not specific or the experience is more important than the time, use the present perfect. For example, compare “I saw that movie last night” (specific time) with “I have seen that movie before” (no specific time).

Another useful tip is to think about the words that often go with each tense. The past simple often uses “yesterday”, “ago”, “last”, and “in” plus a past year. The present perfect often uses “already”, “yet”, “just”, “ever”, “never”, and “since”. For example: “I have just eaten,” or “She hasn''t called yet.” These small words can be your clues.

However, don''t worry if you still feel unsure. It takes practice to master this. Remember that in Brazilian Portuguese, the past tense is often used where English uses the present perfect. For example, you might say “Eu já comi” which can be translated as “I have already eaten” or just “I ate”. The important thing is to think about the connection to the present.

Let''s do a quick check. Imagine you are telling a friend about your life. You can say “I have traveled to many places” because the experience is important, not the exact dates. But if you say “I traveled to Portugal in 2019”, you use the past simple because the time is specific. With practice, you''ll get the hang of it!

Now, let''s test your understanding with some questions.', 'Which sentence uses the past simple correctly?', '["I have seen her yesterday.","I saw her yesterday.","I have saw her yesterday.","I seen her yesterday."]'::jsonb, 1, '[{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"A forma correta do passado simples com tempo específico é ''I saw her yesterday.'' As outras opções ou usam o present perfect com tempo passado específico (incorreto) ou formas verbais erradas.","options":["I have seen her yesterday.","I saw her yesterday.","I have saw her yesterday.","I seen her yesterday."],"prompt":"Which sentence uses the past simple correctly?"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"O present perfect é usado para ações que começaram no passado e continuam no presente. ''She has lived in Brazil since 2010'' indica que ela ainda mora no Brasil. As outras opções têm erro de conjugação ou tempo verbal.","options":["She has lived in Brazil since 2010.","She lived in Brazil since 2010.","She has live in Brazil since 2010.","She lives in Brazil since 2010."],"prompt":"Which sentence uses the present perfect correctly?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Para perguntar sobre experiências de vida, usamos o present perfect com ''ever'' e o particípio passado: ''Have you ever eaten?'' As outras opções usam o passado simples (tempo específico) ou estão gramaticalmente incorretas.","options":["Did you ever eat sushi?","Have you ever eaten sushi?","Have you ever eat sushi?","Do you ever eat sushi?"],"prompt":"Choose the correct sentence to talk about a life experience."},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"A ação de perder as chaves tem relevância no presente (não consigo encontrá-las agora). Portanto, usamos o present perfect ''have lost''. O passado simples seria usado se o tempo fosse mais importante, mas aqui a consequência presente é o foco.","options":["have lost","lost","had lost","lose"],"prompt":"Complete the sentence: ''I ______ my keys this morning. I can''t find them now.''"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
