insert into public.quick_lessons (id, language, level, title, body, question, options, answer_index, questions, sort_order, is_published)
values ('generated-quick-lesson-en-b1-00f2ad6dfd6a', 'en', 'B1', 'If You Study, You Will Learn!', 'Have you ever made plans for the weekend? Maybe you said, "If it rains, I will stay home." Or perhaps you thought, "If I study more, I will get a better job." These are examples of conditional sentences. In English, we use conditionals to talk about possibilities, habits, and imaginary situations. There are three main types that are very useful: zero conditional, first conditional, and second conditional.

The zero conditional is used for facts and general truths. It describes something that always happens in a certain situation. For example: "If you heat water to 100 degrees Celsius, it boils." Here, both parts of the sentence are in the present simple. This structure is simple: ''If'' + present simple, present simple. Brazilians often use this when talking about routines or scientific facts. For example, "If I drink coffee at night, I don''t sleep well."

The first conditional is for real and possible situations in the future. It talks about something that may happen. The structure is: ''If'' + present simple, ''will'' + base verb. For example: "If you study hard, you will pass the exam." This is common when making promises or predictions. You might say, "If we leave now, we will catch the bus." Remember, the ''if'' clause is in the present, not the future. Many learners make the mistake of saying "If it will rain," but we say "If it rains."

The second conditional is for imaginary or unreal situations in the present or future. It describes things that are not true now or are very unlikely. The structure is: ''If'' + past simple, ''would'' + base verb. For example: "If I had a million dollars, I would travel the world." This is not true now, but we imagine it. In everyday life, you might say, "If I were you, I would take that job." Note that we use ''were'' for all subjects in formal English, but ''was'' is also common in informal speech.

It''s important to understand the difference between these conditionals to express yourself clearly. If you confuse them, people might misunderstand your meaning. For instance, if you say "If I have time, I call you" (zero conditional), it means you always do that. But if you say "If I have time, I will call you" (first conditional), it means you are talking about a specific time.

Common mistakes: using ''will'' in the ''if'' clause, mixing up the tenses, or using the wrong conditional for the situation. To avoid these, practice and pay attention to the meaning. Also, remember that ''unless'' means ''if not''. For example, "Unless you hurry, you will be late" is the same as "If you don''t hurry, you will be late."

In short, conditionals are essential for making your English more natural and accurate. With practice, you will use them automatically. So, if you practice every day, you will improve quickly!', 'What is the zero conditional used for?', '["Imaginary situations","General truths and facts","Possible future events","Past regrets"]'::jsonb, 1, '[{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"O zero conditional é usado para verdades gerais e fatos científicos, como ''If you freeze water, it becomes ice''.","options":["Imaginary situations","General truths and facts","Possible future events","Past regrets"],"prompt":"What is the zero conditional used for?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Na primeira condicional, usamos presente simples na cláusula ''if'' e ''will'' na cláusula principal. ''If it rains'' está correto.","options":["If it will rain, we will stay home.","If it rains, we will stay home.","If it rained, we would stay home.","If it rains, we would stay home."],"prompt":"Choose the correct first conditional sentence:"},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"A segunda condicional expressa situações imaginárias ou improváveis, usando passado simples e ''would''.","options":["If you heat ice, it melts.","If I win the lottery, I will buy a car.","If I were rich, I would travel more.","If she comes, we will start."],"prompt":"Which situation uses the second conditional?"},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"O zero conditional usa presente simples nas duas cláusulas: ''If you heat water, it boils''.","options":["If + present simple, will + base verb","If + past simple, would + base verb","If + present simple, present simple","If + present continuous, will + base verb"],"prompt":"What is the correct way to form the zero conditional?"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
