insert into public.quick_lessons (id, language, level, title, body, question, options, answer_index, questions, sort_order, is_published)
values ('generated-quick-lesson-en-b1-ef59744be8db', 'en', 'B1', 'Reporting What People Say: An Introduction to Reported Speech', 'Have you ever told a friend what another person said? For example, you might say, "Maria said she was tired." This is called reported speech or indirect speech. In English, we use reported speech every day to share news, repeat conversations, and tell stories. Learning how to use it correctly will make your English sound more natural and fluent.

When we use reported speech, we often change the tense of the verb. If someone says, "I am hungry," and you report it later, you usually say, "She said she was hungry." The present tense ''am'' changes to the past tense ''was''. This is called backshift. But we don''t always change the tense. If the situation is still true, we can keep the original tense. For example, "He said he likes pizza" (maybe he still likes pizza).

Another important change is with pronouns and time expressions. If Maria says, "I will call you tomorrow," and you report it the next day, you might say, "Maria said she would call me today." Notice how ''I'' becomes ''she'', ''you'' becomes ''me'', and ''tomorrow'' becomes ''today''. These changes depend on the context.

Let''s look at some common verbs used in reported speech: say, tell, ask. Remember that we say something to someone, but we tell someone something. For example, "She said to me, ''I''m happy''" becomes "She told me she was happy." With ''ask'', we report questions: "He asked if I was ready."

Practice is key to mastering reported speech. In this lesson, you will read a short text and answer questions. Then, you will have a grammar section with more details and exercises. Let''s get started!

Read the following text:

Yesterday, I met my friend Ana. She said, "I am moving to São Paulo next month." She told me that she had found a new job there. She asked me if I could help her move. I said, "Of course, I will help you." I told her that I would be free on the weekend. Later, I told my brother about it. He said that he also wanted to help. He said he could borrow a car from a friend. We are all going to meet on Saturday to start packing.

Now, answer the questions below.', 'What did Ana say about moving?', '["She said she is moving to São Paulo next month.","She said she was moving to São Paulo the following month.","She said she moved to São Paulo last month.","She said she would move to São Paulo the previous month."]'::jsonb, 1, '[{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"No texto, Ana disse ''I am moving'', e ao relatar, mudamos para ''was moving'' e ''next month'' vira ''the following month''.","options":["She said she is moving to São Paulo next month.","She said she was moving to São Paulo the following month.","She said she moved to São Paulo last month.","She said she would move to São Paulo the previous month."],"prompt":"What did Ana say about moving?"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"No discurso direto: ''Can you help me move?'' Em discurso indireto, ''can'' vira ''could''.","options":["She asked if I could help her move.","She asked if I would help her move.","She asked if I can help her move.","She asked if I helped her move."],"prompt":"What did Ana ask me?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Como a conversa foi ontem, usamos o passado para relatar: ''was moving''.","options":["I told him that Ana is moving to São Paulo.","I told him that Ana was moving to São Paulo.","I told him that Ana had moved to São Paulo.","I told him that Ana would move to São Paulo."],"prompt":"What did I tell my brother?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"No relato, ''want'' no presente vira ''wanted'' no passado.","options":["He said he wants to help.","He said he wanted to help.","He said he will help.","He said he helps."],"prompt":"What did my brother say?"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
