values ('generated-quick-lesson-en-b1-3f2c16604393', 'en', 'B1', 'Present Perfect vs Past Simple: Making the Right Choice', 'Have you ever wondered when to use ''I have seen'' and when to say ''I saw''? For many Brazilian learners, this is a tricky part of English. But don''t worry! With a little practice, you can master it.

First, let''s talk about the past simple. We use it for actions that started and finished in the past. The time is usually mentioned or understood. For example: ''I visited my grandmother last weekend.'' Here, ''last weekend'' tells us when. Another example: ''She bought a new car in 2020.'' The action is complete and the time is clear.

Now, the present perfect. We use it for past actions that have a connection to now. This connection can be a result in the present or an experience in your life. For example: ''I have lost my keys.'' This means I don''t have them now. Or ''She has visited France.'' This means she has that experience, but we don''t know when. We also use it for actions that started in the past and continue to now, like ''I have lived here for five years.''

A common mistake is to use the present perfect with specific past time expressions like ''yesterday'', ''last week'', or ''in 2010''. For those, we must use the past simple. For example, we say ''I saw him yesterday'', not ''I have seen him yesterday.''

Another mistake is using the past simple for experiences when the time is not important. For instance, ''Have you ever eaten sushi?'' is correct, not ''Did you ever eat sushi?'' unless you''re asking about a specific time in the past.

Let''s practice! Read the sentences and see if you can identify the correct form. Remember: for finished actions with a specific time, use past simple. For experiences and results connected to now, use present perfect.

Here are some questions to test your understanding. Choose the best option for each sentence.', 'Choose the correct sentence:', '["I have seen that movie last night.","I saw that movie last night.","I have saw that movie last night.","I seen that movie last night."]'::jsonb, 1, '[{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Quando há um tempo específico no passado (''last night''), usamos o Past Simple: ''I saw''.","options":["I have seen that movie last night.","I saw that movie last night.","I have saw that movie last night.","I seen that movie last night."],"prompt":"Choose the correct sentence:"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"Usamos Present Perfect para experiências de vida sem tempo específico: ''has been'' indica que ela visitou Paris.","options":["has been","went","has gone","goes"],"prompt":"Complete: ''She ______ to Paris twice.''"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Com ''since'' (desde), usamos Present Perfect: ''I haven''t seen him since Monday.''","options":["I didn''t see him since Monday.","I haven''t seen him since Monday.","I haven''t seen him for Monday.","I didn''t seen him since Monday."],"prompt":"Which sentence is correct?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Com ''yesterday'', tempo específico, usamos Past Simple: ''They finished the project yesterday.''","options":["They have finished the project yesterday.","They finished the project yesterday.","They have finish the project yesterday.","They finishes the project yesterday."],"prompt":"Choose the correct sentence:"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
