values ('generated-quick-lesson-en-b1-f8bfa1790173', 'en', 'B1', 'When the Phone Rang: Past Continuous in Action', 'Imagine you are relaxing at home on a rainy Sunday afternoon. You are reading a good book, and the rain is falling outside. Suddenly, the phone rings. You answer it, and it''s your friend from work. She says, "I was thinking about you! What were you doing?" This is a perfect moment to use the past continuous tense.

The past continuous helps us talk about actions that were in progress at a specific time in the past. It sets the scene and provides background. For example, "I was reading when the phone rang." Here, ''was reading'' is the longer action in progress, and ''rang'' is a shorter action that interrupted it.

To form the past continuous, we use ''was'' or ''were'' + the present participle (verb + -ing). For ''I'', ''he'', ''she'', it: ''was''. For ''you'', ''we'', ''they'': ''were''. For example, ''She was cooking dinner when I arrived.'' The negative is formed by adding ''not'' after ''was/were'': ''They were not (weren''t) sleeping at midnight.'' Questions invert the subject and the auxiliary: ''Were you watching TV at 9 PM?''

We also use the past continuous for two actions happening at the same time in the past. For instance, ''While I was cleaning the house, my husband was fixing the car.'' This shows parallel actions. Sometimes we use ''while'' to connect them.

One common mistake is using the past continuous with verbs that describe states, like ''know'' or ''believe''. We don''t say ''I was knowing the answer.'' Instead, we say ''I knew the answer.'' State verbs usually take the simple past.

Another use is to describe a temporary situation in the past: ''At that time, I was working as a waiter.'' It emphasizes the duration and temporary nature, unlike the simple past which would be ''I worked as a waiter'' (a completed action).

Now, let''s practice with the phone call scenario. You''ll answer questions about what you were doing and what happened. Remember to use the past continuous to describe the background actions and the simple past for the interruptions. This will help you tell stories more vividly and naturally.

By mastering the past continuous, you''ll be able to narrate past events with more detail and texture. So, next time someone asks ''What were you doing when...?'' you''ll be ready to respond with confidence.', 'What is the past continuous tense used for?', '["To describe actions that were in progress at a specific time in the past.","To describe completed actions in the past.","To describe future plans.","To describe habitual actions in the present."]'::jsonb, 0, '[{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"O past continuous é usado para descrever ações que estavam em andamento em um momento específico no passado, como em ''I was reading when the phone rang''.","options":["To describe actions that were in progress at a specific time in the past.","To describe completed actions in the past.","To describe future plans.","To describe habitual actions in the present."],"prompt":"What is the past continuous tense used for?"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"A estrutura ''was + watching'' indica uma ação contínua no passado, o que caracteriza o past continuous.","options":["I was watching TV at 8 PM.","I watched TV at 8 PM.","I will watch TV at 8 PM.","I watch TV at 8 PM."],"prompt":"Which sentence is in the past continuous?"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"Para o sujeito ''they'', usamos ''were'' + verbo com -ing. Portanto, ''were sleeping'' é a forma correta.","options":["were sleeping","was sleeping","slept","sleep"],"prompt":"Choose the correct form: \"They ______ when the alarm went off.\""},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"A forma negativa do past continuous é ''was not'' ou ''were not'', que pode ser contraído para ''wasn''t'' ou ''weren''t''. Aqui, ''She wasn''t working'' é correto.","options":["She wasn''t working yesterday.","She didn''t was working yesterday.","She isn''t working yesterday.","She don''t work yesterday."],"prompt":"Which sentence correctly uses the negative form?"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
