insert into public.quick_lessons (id, language, level, title, body, question, options, answer_index, questions, sort_order, is_published)
values ('generated-quick-lesson-en-b1-24772f286ecf', 'en', 'B1', 'Passive Voice Basics: Understanding and Using It in English', 'Have you ever noticed how some sentences focus more on the action than on the person doing it? For example, when we say ''The cake was eaten by the children,'' we are more interested in the cake and the action of eating than in the children themselves. This is the passive voice. In English, we use the passive voice when the action is more important than who did it, or when we don''t know who did it. It is very common in news reports, scientific writing, and formal situations. For instance, in a news report, you might hear ''The new bridge was opened yesterday'' because the focus is on the bridge, not on who opened it.

In this quick lesson, you will learn how to form and use the passive voice in the present simple and past simple tenses. This is a basic level, so we will focus on the most common structures. Understanding the passive voice will help you read and write more clearly, especially in academic or professional contexts.

Let''s start with the structure. In the passive voice, we use the verb ''to be'' in the same tense as the active sentence, and the main verb in the past participle form. For example, in the present simple, we say ''is/are + past participle''. So, ''The book is read by many students.'' In the past simple, we say ''was/were + past participle''. For example, ''The book was read by the class yesterday.''

To change an active sentence to passive, we move the object to the subject position. For example, in the active sentence ''John writes a letter,'' the object is ''a letter''. In the passive, it becomes ''A letter is written by John''. We can also omit the ''by'' phrase if we don''t know who did it or if it is obvious. For instance, ''The house was built in 1900'' – we don''t say by whom.

Now, let''s see why we use the passive voice. First, we use it when the doer is unknown or unimportant. For example, ''My wallet was stolen.'' We don''t know who stole it. Second, we use it when the action itself is more important. For example, in science, we say ''The experiment was conducted carefully.'' Third, we use it to be polite or formal, like in official notices: ''Please be seated.''

However, be careful: not all verbs can be used in the passive. Only transitive verbs, which take an object, can be passive. For example, ''happen'' is an intransitive verb, so we cannot say ''The accident was happened.'' Instead, we say ''The accident happened.''

Let''s practice with some common mistakes. Many learners say ''The window was broke'' instead of ''The window was broken.'' The past participle of ''break'' is ''broken''. Also, ''The letter was wrote'' is wrong; it should be ''The letter was written.''

Now, let''s look at some examples in context:

- Active: The company produces eco-friendly products. Passive: Eco-friendly products are produced by the company.
- Active: The teacher explained the lesson. Passive: The lesson was explained by the teacher.

Remember, you can also use the passive with modal verbs, but that is for a higher level. For now, focus on the present and past simple.

To sum up: The passive voice is used to emphasize the action or when the doer is unknown. Form it with the correct form of ''to be'' + past participle. Practice by rewriting sentences from active to passive and vice versa. This will make your English more flexible and precise.

Now, test your understanding with the questions below.
', 'Which sentence is in the passive voice?', '["The chef cooked a delicious meal.","A delicious meal was cooked by the chef.","The chef is cooking a delicious meal.","The chef will cook a delicious meal."]'::jsonb, 1, '[{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"A frase ''A delicious meal was cooked by the chef'' está na voz passiva, pois o foco está na refeição (objeto) e o verbo ''was cooked'' é formado por ''was'' (passado do verbo ''to be'') + particípio passado ''cooked''. As outras opções estão na voz ativa.","options":["The chef cooked a delicious meal.","A delicious meal was cooked by the chef.","The chef is cooking a delicious meal.","The chef will cook a delicious meal."],"prompt":"Which sentence is in the passive voice?"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"No presente simples, a voz passiva é formada com o verbo ''to be'' no presente (am/is/are) + particípio passado do verbo principal. Exemplo: ''The book is read.'' As outras opções usam tempos verbais diferentes.","options":["am/is/are + past participle","was/were + past participle","have/has + past participle","will be + past participle"],"prompt":"How is the passive voice formed in the present simple?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"A frase original está no passado simples (built), então a passiva deve usar ''was/were'' + particípio passado. O particípio de ''build'' é ''built''. Portanto, ''This bridge was built in 1990.'' A opção (a) está no presente, (c) falta o verbo ''to be'', e (d) usa ''builded'' que não existe.","options":["This bridge is built in 1990.","This bridge was built in 1990.","This bridge built in 1990.","This bridge was builded in 1990."],"prompt":"Choose the correct passive sentence for: ''They built this bridge in 1990.''"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Na voz passiva, o foco está na ação ou no objeto, não no agente. Em notícias, muitas vezes o agente é desconhecido ou não é importante, então a passiva é usada. Exemplo: ''The money was stolen.'' As outras opções não são razões para usar a passiva.","options":["To make the sentences longer.","Because the doer is unknown or unimportant.","To show who did the action.","To use more verbs."],"prompt":"Why do we often use the passive voice in news reports?"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
