insert into public.quick_lessons (id, language, level, title, body, question, options, answer_index, questions, sort_order, is_published)
values ('generated-quick-lesson-en-b1-6f663f77044c', 'en', 'B1', 'What Would You Do? Mastering Conditionals in English', 'Conditional sentences are a powerful tool in English. They allow us to talk about possibilities, give advice, and imagine different outcomes. In this lesson, we will focus on the zero, first, and second conditionals, which are essential for everyday communication.

The zero conditional is used for universal truths and habits. It expresses that when a certain condition is present, a specific result always follows. For example, "If you heat water to 100°C, it boils." The structure is: if + present simple, present simple. This conditional is not about a particular time but about general facts.

The first conditional is used for real future possibilities. It describes a likely result of a particular action or event. For instance, "If it rains tomorrow, we will cancel the picnic." The structure is: if + present simple, will + base verb. This form is common for making promises, warnings, and predictions.

The second conditional is used for unreal or hypothetical situations in the present or future. It helps us talk about imaginary scenarios and give advice. For example, "If I were you, I would take that job." The structure is: if + past simple, would + base verb. Note that with the second conditional, we use "were" for all subjects after "if" (though in informal English, "was" is often used).

Understanding these conditionals enriches your ability to express ideas clearly. The zero conditional states facts, the first conditional deals with real future outcomes, and the second conditional explores unreal possibilities or offers advice. Each has a distinct form and function.

Here are some common mistakes to avoid: mixing forms (e.g., "If it will rain"), using "would" in the if-clause, and forgetting the comma when the if-clause starts the sentence. Also, remember that the zero conditional is not used for specific times but for general truths.

Now, let''s practice with these examples in the questions below.', 'Which sentence correctly uses the zero conditional?', '["If you will mix red and blue, you get purple.","If you mix red and blue, you get purple.","If you mixed red and blue, you would get purple.","If you mix red and blue, you will get purple."]'::jsonb, 1, '[{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"O zero conditional usa presente simples nas duas partes para verdades universais. As outras opções usam formas de primeiro ou segundo condicional.","options":["If you will mix red and blue, you get purple.","If you mix red and blue, you get purple.","If you mixed red and blue, you would get purple.","If you mix red and blue, you will get purple."],"prompt":"Which sentence correctly uses the zero conditional?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"O primeiro condicional usa presente simples após ''if'' e ''will'' + verbo na oração principal.","options":["If it rains tomorrow, we cancel the picnic.","If it rains tomorrow, we will cancel the picnic.","If it rained tomorrow, we would cancel the picnic.","If it will rain tomorrow, we cancel the picnic."],"prompt":"Which sentence correctly uses the first conditional?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Situações hipotéticas usam o segundo condicional: ''had'' + ''would travel''.","options":["have / will travel","had / would travel","had / will travel","have / would travel"],"prompt":"If I ___ a million dollars, I ___ around the world."},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"Conselho e situações irreais usam o segundo condicional, como ''If I were you...''.","options":["If you heat ice, it melts.","If you study, you will pass the test.","If I were you, I would talk to her.","If it snows tomorrow, we will make a snowman."],"prompt":"Which situation uses the second conditional?"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
