insert into public.quick_lessons (id, language, level, title, body, question, options, answer_index, questions, sort_order, is_published)
values ('generated-quick-lesson-en-b1-addd837ada35', 'en', 'B1', 'Reporting What Others Say: Introduction to Reported Speech', 'In English, we often need to tell someone what another person said. We can do this in two ways: using direct speech or reported speech. Direct speech repeats the exact words, usually with quotation marks. For example, Maria said, "I am tired." Reported speech, also called indirect speech, does not use quotation marks and often changes the verb tense and pronouns. For example, Maria said (that) she was tired.

When we use reported speech, we usually change the verb tense in the reported clause. If the reporting verb (like ''said'' or ''told'') is in the past, we often shift the tense back. Simple present becomes simple past, present continuous becomes past continuous, and simple past becomes past perfect. For instance, "I am happy" becomes "She said she was happy." "I am studying" becomes "He said he was studying." "I visited my grandparents" becomes "She said she had visited her grandparents."

We also need to change pronouns and possessive adjectives to match the new perspective. For example, if John says, "I like my job," we report it as "John said he liked his job." The pronoun ''I'' changes to ''he'' and ''my'' changes to ''his''.

Time and place expressions often change as well. ''Now'' becomes ''then'', ''today'' becomes ''that day'', ''tomorrow'' becomes ''the next day'' or ''the following day'', and ''yesterday'' becomes ''the day before''. For example, "I will call you tomorrow" becomes "She said she would call me the next day."

It is important to remember that reported speech is very common in conversations, news reports, and storytelling. Mastering it helps you share information accurately and politely. You might also use it to report questions, but we will focus on statements first.

Now, let''s practice with some examples. Imagine your friend Ana says to you, "I love Brazilian music." Later, you tell another friend: "Ana said she loved Brazilian music." Even if she still loves it, we often use the past tense because the reporting verb is in the past.

Another example: Your boss says, "We need to finish the project by Friday." You report: "The boss said we needed to finish the project by Friday." Notice that ''need'' becomes ''needed'' and ''we'' stays the same if you are included. If you are reporting to someone else, you might say "he said we needed..." or "he said they needed..." depending on the situation.

Finally, remember that we often use ''that'' after the reporting verb, but it is optional. For example, "She said that she was tired" or "She said she was tired" are both correct. In informal speech, we usually omit ''that''.

Now, test your understanding with the following questions.', 'What is reported speech?', '["Repeating exact words with quotation marks","Telling someone what another person said, often with changes","Speaking loudly to a group","Writing a dialogue between two people"]'::jsonb, 1, '[{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Reported speech (ou discurso indireto) é quando contamos o que outra pessoa disse, geralmente mudando o tempo verbal e os pronomes. A primeira opção se refere ao discurso direto.","options":["Repeating exact words with quotation marks","Telling someone what another person said, often with changes","Speaking loudly to a group","Writing a dialogue between two people"],"prompt":"What is reported speech?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Na reported speech, o presente simples (''am'') geralmente muda para o passado simples (''was''). Portanto, a opção correta é ''He said he was happy.''","options":["He said he is happy.","He said he was happy.","He said I was happy.","He said he had been happy."],"prompt":"Choose the correct reported speech for: ''I am happy.''"},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"Em geral, ''tomorrow'' vira ''the next day'' (ou ''the following day''). As outras opções não são usadas nesse contexto.","options":["to the day after","to today","to the next day","to yesterday"],"prompt":"How do we often change ''tomorrow'' in reported speech?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Quando o verbo de elocução está no passado, o ''will'' geralmente vira ''would''. Portanto, a opção correta é ''She said she would come to the party.''","options":["She said she will come to the party.","She said she would come to the party.","She said she comes to the party.","She said she came to the party."],"prompt":"Which sentence is correct reported speech?"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
