insert into public.quick_lessons (id, language, level, title, body, question, options, answer_index, questions, sort_order, is_published)
values ('generated-quick-lesson-en-b1-32824f6e7280', 'en', 'B1', 'Friends and Relatives: Understanding Defining and Non-Defining Relative Clauses', 'Have you ever tried to describe a person or a thing but felt your sentence was missing something? In English, we often use relative clauses to add extra information about someone or something. Relative clauses are like little packages of information that we attach to a noun. They usually begin with words like ''who,'' ''which,'' ''that,'' or ''where.'' For example, in the sentence ''The woman who lives next door is a doctor,'' the clause ''who lives next door'' tells us exactly which woman we are talking about. This is called a defining relative clause. It is essential because without it, we would not know which woman the speaker means.

Defining relative clauses do not use commas. They give necessary information to identify the person or thing. For instance, ''The book that I borrowed from you is great.'' Here, the clause ''that I borrowed from you'' identifies which book. If we remove it, the sentence ''The book is great'' could refer to any book. Therefore, the clause is essential for the meaning.

On the other hand, non-defining relative clauses add extra, non-essential information. They are set off by commas. For example, ''My brother, who lives in São Paulo, is an engineer.'' The clause ''who lives in São Paulo'' provides extra detail, but even without it, we still know which brother we mean. In written English, you must use commas with non-defining clauses. Also, you cannot use ''that'' in non-defining clauses; you must use ''who'' for people and ''which'' for things. For example, ''The car, which is red, belongs to my neighbor'' is correct, but ''The car, that is red, belongs to my neighbor'' is not.

When do we choose between ''who,'' ''which,'' and ''where''? Use ''who'' for people, ''which'' for things, and ''where'' for places. For example, ''The city where I was born is beautiful.'' In defining clauses, you can sometimes use ''that'' instead of ''who'' or ''which.'' For instance, ''The girl that won the prize is my friend'' is fine. However, in non-defining clauses, you must use ''who'' or ''which,'' not ''that.''

One common mistake is using commas with defining clauses. Remember: no commas for essential information. Another mistake is using ''which'' for people. Always use ''who'' for people. Also, be careful with the pronouns. In non-defining clauses, you cannot omit the relative pronoun. For example, you can say ''The man I met'' (defining, pronoun omitted), but you cannot say ''My father, I admire, is kind.'' You must say ''My father, whom I admire, is kind'' (even though ''whom'' is formal). At B1 level, you can use ''who'' instead of ''whom'' in informal speech.

Now, let''s practice with some examples. Imagine you are describing your family and friends. You can say, ''My cousin, who lives in Rio, is a musician.'' Or ''The teacher that helped me is very patient.'' These clauses make your English more natural and detailed.

Remember the key differences: defining clauses are essential, no commas, and can use ''that.'' Non-defining clauses are extra, use commas, and cannot use ''that.'' Also, use ''who'' for people, ''which'' for things, and ''where'' for places. With these rules, you will be able to express yourself more precisely and interestingly.

Let''s test your understanding with a few questions. Choose the correct option for each sentence. Good luck!', 'Choose the correct relative pronoun to complete the sentence: ''The woman ______ lives next door is a doctor.''', '["which","who","that","where"]'::jsonb, 1, '[{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Usamos ''who'' para pessoas, pois estamos nos referindo à mulher. ''Which'' é para coisas, ''that'' pode ser usado em defining clauses, mas ''who'' é mais apropriado para pessoas, e ''where'' é para lugares.","options":["which","who","that","where"],"prompt":"Choose the correct relative pronoun to complete the sentence: ''The woman ______ lives next door is a doctor.''"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"Non-defining relative clauses usam vírgulas e não podem usar ''that''. A opção correta tem vírgulas e usa ''who''.","options":["My brother who lives in São Paulo is an engineer.","My brother, that lives in São Paulo, is an engineer.","My brother which lives in São Paulo is an engineer."],"prompt":"Choose the correct sentence with a non-defining relative clause."},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"A opção 3 é uma defining clause porque é essencial para identificar o livro e não tem vírgulas. As outras têm vírgulas, indicando non-defining.","options":["The car, which is red, belongs to my neighbor.","My sister, who lives in Paris, is coming to visit.","The book that I borrowed from you is great.","The city, where I was born, is beautiful."],"prompt":"Which sentence has a defining relative clause?"},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"A opção correta é uma defining clause sem vírgulas usando ''who'' para pessoas. ''Which'' é para coisas, e essencial não usa vírgulas.","options":["The teacher which helped me is very patient.","The teacher, that helped me, is very patient.","The teacher who helped me is very patient."],"prompt":"Choose the correct sentence:"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
