insert into public.quick_lessons (id, language, level, title, body, question, options, answer_index, questions, sort_order, is_published)
values ('generated-quick-lesson-en-b1-76ac24842658', 'en', 'B1', 'Enjoying the Process: Verbs + -ing or To', 'Every day, we make choices about how to spend our time. Some activities we really enjoy, and others we simply need to do. In English, the way we talk about these activities often depends on the verb we use. For example, we can say ''I enjoy reading'' or ''I want to read''. Did you notice the difference? After ''enjoy'', we use the -ing form (reading), but after ''want'', we use ''to'' plus the base verb (to read). This is one of the most common patterns in English, and it is important for expressing preferences, plans, and habits.

First, let''s look at verbs that are followed by the gerund (the -ing form). These verbs often describe feelings or attitudes. For instance, ''like'', ''love'', ''hate'', ''dislike'', ''enjoy'', and ''avoid'' all take the gerund. So, we say ''I love swimming'' and ''She avoids driving at night''. Another group includes ''finish'', ''consider'', ''suggest'', ''imagine'', and ''keep''. For example, ''He finished writing the report'' and ''They considered moving to another city''. Remember, the gerund acts like a noun, so it can be the subject of a sentence too, like ''Swimming is good for you''.

Now, let''s talk about verbs followed by the infinitive (with ''to''). These verbs often express goals, desires, or decisions. Examples include ''want'', ''need'', ''decide'', ''hope'', ''plan'', ''promise'', ''learn'', and ''offer''. So, we say ''We need to buy some food'' and ''She hopes to travel more''. The infinitive is also used after adjectives, like ''It is important to study'' and ''I am happy to help''. In addition, some verbs can be followed by either a gerund or an infinitive, but the meaning may change. For example, ''I remember locking the door'' (I have a memory of doing it) versus ''I remembered to lock the door'' (I didn''t forget to do it). Similarly, ''I stopped smoking'' (I quit the habit) versus ''I stopped to smoke'' (I paused a journey to have a cigarette).

Another useful pattern is verb + object + infinitive. With verbs like ''tell'', ''ask'', ''want'', and ''encourage'', we use a person and then the infinitive. For instance, ''My boss told me to finish the project'' and ''They asked us to arrive early''. This pattern is common in work and school situations. Also, we often use the infinitive to explain the purpose of an action, like ''I went to the store to buy milk''. Here, ''to buy'' tells us why we went.

To practice, think about your daily routine. What do you enjoy doing? What do you want to do? For example, you might say ''I enjoy listening to music'' or ''I want to learn English''. Notice that you also need to use the correct form after prepositions. After a preposition, we always use the gerund, like ''I am interested in reading'' or ''She is good at cooking''. This is a fixed rule.

In summary, choosing between the gerund and the infinitive often depends on the main verb. Some verbs are always followed by one or the other, and some change meaning. To improve, keep a list of these verbs and practice making sentences. Listen to English songs and watch videos to see how these patterns are used in real life. With time, using the correct form will become natural. Remember, it''s not just about knowing the rules; it''s about enjoying the journey of learning. So, what do you want to do today?', 'Which sentence is correct?', '["I want going home.","I enjoy to go home.","I want to go home.","I enjoy go home."]'::jsonb, 2, '[{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"O verbo ''want'' é seguido de infinitivo com ''to'', então ''I want to go home'' é correto. As outras opções usam formas incorretas.","options":["I want going home.","I enjoy to go home.","I want to go home.","I enjoy go home."],"prompt":"Which sentence is correct?"},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"''avoid'' é seguido de gerúndio (-ing), então ''walking'' é a forma correta.","options":["walk","to walk","walking","walks"],"prompt":"Complete: She avoids ______ alone at night."},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"''remember + gerúndio'' indica memória de uma ação passada. Portanto, a frase significa que a pessoa lembra de ter trancado a porta.","options":["I forgot to lock the door.","I have a memory of locking the door.","I will lock the door later.","I need to lock the door."],"prompt":"Choose the sentence with the correct meaning: ''I remember locking the door.''"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"Depois de preposição, usamos gerúndio. ''Interested in'' é uma expressão fixa, então ''learning'' é a forma correta.","options":["He is interested in learning English.","He is interested in learn English.","He is interested to learn English.","He is interested learn English."],"prompt":"Which sentence is correct?"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
