insert into public.quick_lessons (id, language, level, title, body, question, options, answer_index, questions, sort_order, is_published)
values ('generated-quick-lesson-en-b1-d4edbb1168f3', 'en', 'B1', 'Everyday Phrasal Verbs for B1 Learners', 'Phrasal verbs are a big part of everyday English. They are made of a verb and a small word, like ''up'' or ''out''. For example, ''get up'' means to leave your bed in the morning. At B1 level, you will meet many of these. They are very common in conversations, so learning them will help you sound more natural.

One common phrasal verb is ''look after''. It means to take care of someone or something. For example, "She looks after her little brother on weekends." Another is ''find out'', which means to discover information. You can say, "I need to find out when the train leaves."

Another useful one is ''give up''. It means to stop trying or to stop doing something. For example, "He gave up smoking last year." Also, ''run out of'' means to have no more of something. Like, "We ran out of milk, so I went to the store."

Then there is ''put off'', which means to delay something. For example, "They put off the meeting until next week." And ''turn on'' means to start a machine or light. You might say, "Please turn on the TV."

These phrasal verbs are used in many everyday situations. In a work context, you might ''carry out'' a plan, which means to do it. Or you might ''deal with'' a problem, which means to handle it. Learning these will help you in conversations at work or with friends.

One good way to remember them is to practice with examples. Try to make your own sentences. Also, you can listen to songs or watch shows in English. This helps you see how they are used in real life. Remember, phrasal verbs can be tricky because the meaning is not always obvious. But with practice, you will get used to them.

In the next lesson, we will look at more phrasal verbs and how to use them correctly. For now, try to notice them when you hear or read English. This will help you learn faster.', 'What does ''look after'' mean?', '["To search for something","To take care of someone or something","To look at something quickly","To stop doing something"]'::jsonb, 1, '[{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"''Look after'' significa cuidar de alguém ou algo. Por exemplo, ''She looks after her little brother'' quer dizer que ela cuida do irmão mais novo.","options":["To search for something","To take care of someone or something","To look at something quickly","To stop doing something"],"prompt":"What does ''look after'' mean?"},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"''Find out'' significa descobrir uma informação. Por exemplo, ''I need to find out the truth'' significa que você precisa descobrir a verdade.","options":["Give up","Run out of","Find out","Put off"],"prompt":"Which phrasal verb means ''to discover information''?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"''Run out of'' significa ficar sem algo, não ter mais. Exemplo: ''We ran out of milk'' = ficamos sem leite.","options":["To escape quickly","To have no more of something","To go for a run","To throw something away"],"prompt":"What does ''run out of'' mean?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"''Put off'' significa adiar. A alternativa correta é ''She put off her homework for later'' (ela adiou a lição de casa para depois). As outras usam verbos diferentes.","options":["I put off my shoes at the door.","She put off her homework for later.","He put off the lights before leaving.","They put off the party last night."],"prompt":"Which sentence uses ''put off'' correctly?"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
