insert into public.quick_lessons (id, language, level, title, body, question, options, answer_index, questions, sort_order, is_published)
values ('generated-quick-lesson-en-b1-6570bb85e541', 'en', 'B1', 'Present Perfect vs Past Simple: Making the Right Choice', 'Have you ever wondered when to use the present perfect or the past simple in English? Many Brazilian learners find this confusing, but the rules are simpler than you think. The past simple is for finished actions at a specific time in the past. The present perfect connects the past to the present. Let''s explore the differences with clear examples.

First, think about time expressions. With the past simple, we often use words like ''yesterday'', ''last week'', ''in 2010'', or ''two days ago''. These point to a definite time that is finished. For example, ''I visited my grandmother last Sunday.'' The action is complete and the time is clear. With the present perfect, we use words like ''already'', ''yet'', ''ever'', ''never'', or ''since''. These do not say exactly when. For example, ''I have already visited my grandmother this week.'' The exact time is not important.

Second, consider the result or consequence. The present perfect often shows a result in the present. For example, ''I have lost my keys'' means I don''t have them now. The past simple only tells about the past: ''I lost my keys yesterday'' – maybe I found them later. The present perfect asks if an experience has happened in your life: ''Have you ever eaten feijoada?'' The past simple asks about a specific past event: ''Did you eat feijoada at the party last night?''

Third, be careful with ''for'' and ''since''. We use ''for'' with a duration (for two years, for a long time) and ''since'' with a starting point (since 2020, since I was a child). Both often go with the present perfect to talk about actions that started in the past and continue now. For example, ''I have lived in São Paulo for ten years'' (and I still live there). But if the action is finished, use past simple: ''I lived in São Paulo for ten years'' (but I moved away).

Fourth, remember the difference between ''gone'' and ''been''. ''He has gone to Rio'' means he is there now. ''He has been to Rio'' means he visited and came back. With the past simple, we just say ''He went to Rio last month'' – we don''t know if he is back. The present perfect is also common with recent news or changes: ''The company has opened a new office'' (news). We use the past simple to give details: ''The company opened the office in March.''

Finally, in American English, we often use the past simple with ''just'', ''already'', and ''yet'', but in British English, the present perfect is preferred. For example, ''I just saw her'' (American) versus ''I have just seen her'' (British). Both are correct, but you should be consistent. Also, remember that ''ever'' and ''never'' are common in questions and negative sentences with the present perfect.

In summary, use the past simple for finished actions at a specific time. Use the present perfect for life experiences, recent actions with present results, and durations that continue to now. Practice with real situations, and soon you will choose the correct tense without thinking. Now, test your understanding with the questions below.', 'Which sentence uses the past simple correctly?', '["I have seen that movie yesterday.","I saw that movie yesterday.","I have saw that movie yesterday.","I seen that movie yesterday."]'::jsonb, 1, '[{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"A opção correta usa o passado simples com ''yesterday'' (tempo específico). ''Have seen'' não combina com ''yesterday'', e ''saw'' é o passado de ''see''.","options":["I have seen that movie yesterday.","I saw that movie yesterday.","I have saw that movie yesterday.","I seen that movie yesterday."],"prompt":"Which sentence uses the past simple correctly?"},{"answer_index":0,"explanation_pt_br":"Usamos o present perfect com ''since'' para indicar o início de uma ação que continua até o presente. ''She has lived'' está correto.","options":["She has lived here since 2015.","She lived here since 2015.","She has live here since 2015.","She lives here since 2015."],"prompt":"Choose the correct sentence:"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"O passado simples é usado para ações concluídas num momento específico do passado. As outras opções descrevem usos do present perfect.","options":["For actions that started in the past and continue now","For finished actions at a specific time in the past","For actions that have a result in the present","For recent changes or news"],"prompt":"When do we use the past simple?"},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"Para experiências de vida, usamos o present perfect com ''never'' e o particípio passado ''eaten''.","options":["I have never ate sushi.","I never ate sushi.","I have never eaten sushi.","I never have eaten sushi."],"prompt":"Which sentence is correct?"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
