insert into public.quick_lessons (id, language, level, title, body, question, options, answer_index, questions, sort_order, is_published)
values ('generated-quick-lesson-en-b1-f6443bf6f80c', 'en', 'B1', 'What Did She Say? Introduction to Reported Speech', 'Have you ever tried to tell a friend what someone else said? In English, we often need to do this. For example, you might say, ''Maria said she was tired.'' This is called reported speech. It is a way to talk about what someone said without using their exact words. In this lesson, you will learn the basics of reported speech.

First, let''s understand the difference between direct speech and reported speech. Direct speech uses the exact words a person said, and we put them in quotation marks. For example: Anna said, ''I am happy.'' Reported speech, on the other hand, does not use quotation marks. We change the sentence structure. Instead of Anna said, ''I am happy,'' we say, ''Anna said she was happy.'' Notice that the verb ''am'' changes to ''was''. This is called backshift.

Backshift is important. When we report something that was said in the past, we usually move the verb one step back in time. For example, if someone says, ''I work in a bank,'' we report it as, ''He said he worked in a bank.'' The present simple ''work'' becomes past simple ''worked''. Similarly, if someone says, ''I am going to São Paulo,'' we report it as, ''She said she was going to São Paulo.'' The present continuous ''am going'' becomes past continuous ''was going''.

But what about modal verbs? They also change. For example, ''will'' becomes ''would'', and ''can'' becomes ''could''. If your friend says, ''I will help you,'' you can report it as, ''My friend said he would help me.'' If someone says, ''I can speak three languages,'' you can report it as, ''She said she could speak three languages.'' These changes are important for correct grammar.

However, there are some exceptions. If the reporting verb is in the present tense (like ''says''), we do not change the tense. For example, ''Anna says she is happy.'' The tense remains the same. Also, if the reported statement is a general truth or fact, we do not change the tense. For example, ''The teacher said that the Earth is round.'' The present simple ''is'' stays because the fact is still true.

Now, let''s talk about pronouns and time expressions. We also need to change pronouns to match the new perspective. For example, if Maria says, ''I like my job,'' you report it as, ''Maria said she liked her job.'' The pronoun ''I'' changes to ''she'', and ''my'' becomes ''her''. Time expressions often change too. If someone says, ''I will call you tomorrow,'' and you report it the next day, you might say, ''He said he would call me today.'' ''Tomorrow'' becomes ''today'' because the time changed.

Practice is key to mastering reported speech. Try to notice how you report things in your daily life. When you tell a friend what the teacher said, or what your boss said, you are using reported speech. With practice, it will become natural. Remember, the main idea is to communicate the message clearly, even if the exact words change.', 'What is reported speech?', '["Using the exact words someone said with quotation marks.","Talking about what someone said without using their exact words.","A type of speech that is very formal.","A way to report news on TV."]'::jsonb, 1, '[{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Reported speech é quando falamos sobre o que alguém disse sem usar as palavras exatas, sem aspas.","options":["Using the exact words someone said with quotation marks.","Talking about what someone said without using their exact words.","A type of speech that is very formal.","A way to report news on TV."],"prompt":"What is reported speech?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"No reported speech, o presente simples ''eat'' vira passado simples ''ate''. Então a opção correta é ''He said he ate rice every day.''","options":["He said he eats rice every day.","He said he ate rice every day.","He said he has eaten rice every day.","He said he is eating rice every day."],"prompt":"If someone says, ''I eat rice every day,'' how do you report it?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"A opção correta é ''Ana said she was from Brazil.'' porque usa reported speech sem aspas e com backshift do verbo.","options":["Ana said, ''I am from Brazil.''","Ana said she was from Brazil.","Ana said, ''I was from Brazil.''","Ana said that I am from Brazil."],"prompt":"Which sentence is an example of reported speech?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Não mudamos o tempo verbal quando a declaração é uma verdade geral ou fato, como ''The Earth is round.''","options":["When the reporting verb is in the past.","When the statement is a general truth.","When the speaker is a woman.","When the sentence is very short."],"prompt":"Why do we sometimes NOT change the tense in reported speech?"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
