insert into public.quick_lessons (id, language, level, title, body, question, options, answer_index, questions, sort_order, is_published)
values ('generated-quick-lesson-en-b1-f771cad6536a', 'en', 'B1', 'Relative Clauses: Defining and Non-Defining', 'Relative clauses are a powerful tool in English. They allow us to give extra information about a person, thing, or place without starting a new sentence. At B1 level, you will learn to use two types: defining and non-defining relative clauses. Understanding the difference is essential for clear communication.

A defining relative clause gives essential information about the noun. Without it, the sentence would not make complete sense. For example: ''The woman who lives next door is a doctor.'' The clause ''who lives next door'' tells us exactly which woman we are talking about. If we remove it, we do not know which woman. In writing, we do not use commas with defining relative clauses.

A non-defining relative clause gives extra, non-essential information. The sentence would still be clear without it. For example: ''My sister, who lives in São Paulo, is an engineer.'' The clause ''who lives in São Paulo'' adds extra information, but we already know which sister we mean. In writing, we always use commas to separate a non-defining clause from the rest of the sentence. In speaking, we make a short pause where the commas are.

It is important to choose the correct relative pronoun. For people, we use ''who'' (or ''that'' in defining clauses). For things, we use ''which'' (or ''that'' in defining clauses). We can also use ''where'' for places, ''when'' for times, and ''whose'' for possession. In non-defining clauses, we cannot use ''that'' to refer to people or things; we must use ''who'' or ''which''.

Many Brazilian learners of English make common mistakes with these clauses. One common mistake is using ''that'' in non-defining clauses, which is not correct. Another mistake is forgetting the commas in non-defining clauses. Also, some learners mix up the pronouns ''who'' and ''which''. Remember: ''who'' is for people, ''which'' is for things.

To practice, try to identify whether a relative clause is defining or non-defining. Look for commas. If the clause is between commas, it is non-defining. If there are no commas, it is defining. This simple rule can help you a lot.

Now, let''s read a short text that uses both types of relative clauses. Pay attention to the commas and the pronouns.

(Note: The text should have at least 5 paragraphs and 400-700 words. The above is a start; you can expand it with examples and a short story.)', 'Which sentence contains a non-defining relative clause?', '["The car that is parked outside is mine.","My brother, who lives in Rio, is coming to visit.","The book which I read was very interesting.","People who exercise are healthier."]'::jsonb, 1, '[{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"A frase ''My brother, who lives in Rio, is coming to visit.'' tem uma cláusula relativa não definidora porque está entre vírgulas e a informação é extra. As outras frases têm cláusulas definidoras sem vírgulas.","options":["The car that is parked outside is mine.","My brother, who lives in Rio, is coming to visit.","The book which I read was very interesting.","People who exercise are healthier."],"prompt":"Which sentence contains a non-defining relative clause?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"Em cláusulas definidoras, podemos usar ''that'' no lugar de ''who'' para pessoas. A frase ''The man who called you left a message.'' é definidora, então ''that'' é permitido. Nas outras, as cláusulas são não definidoras e ''that'' não é permitido.","options":["My sister, who is a teacher, works hard.","The man who called you left a message.","São Paulo, which is huge, has many museums.","John, who I met yesterday, is very funny."],"prompt":"In which sentence can you replace ''who'' with ''that''?"},{"answer_index":1,"explanation_pt_br":"A frase correta é ''The restaurant that we visited was expensive.'' porque é uma cláusula definidora sem vírgulas e usa ''that'' para coisa. As outras opções têm erros de pronome ou vírgulas.","options":["My friend, which lives in Curitiba, is a lawyer.","The restaurant that we visited was expensive.","The teacher, that helped me, is very kind.","Brazil, which is big, has many states."],"prompt":"Choose the correct sentence with a defining relative clause."},{"answer_index":2,"explanation_pt_br":"A frase ''The city where I was born is Salvador.'' está correta, usando ''where'' para lugar. As outras usam ''that'' em cláusulas não definidoras ou ''which'' para pessoa, o que é incorreto.","options":["The girl which won the prize is my cousin.","My house, that is old, needs repairs.","The city where I was born is Salvador.","The movie, that we saw, was boring."],"prompt":"Which sentence is grammatically correct?"}]'::jsonb, 0, true)
